 or recent release from a detention facility", "strength": "moderate"}, {"text": "Male sex with men", "strength": "moderate"}], "comorbidities": ["Gonorrhea or other concurrent STI", "Bacterial vaginosis", "HIV infection"], "family_history": [], "social_history": ["Often multiple or recent partners", "May be in a new relationship or a relationship they believe is monogamous", "May not use condoms consistently", "May have limited access to routine preventive care"], "exposures": ["Unprotected vaginal, anal, or oral sex with an infected partner", "Sexual contact with a partner who has urethral discharge or dysuria", "Passage through an infected birth canal (neonates)"], "risk_medications": []}, "presentation": {"chief_complaints": [{"text": "I don't really have any symptoms — my partner told me they tested positive", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "It burns when I pee", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "I've had some discharge down there that isn't normal for me", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "My lower belly has been hurting", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "There's been some bleeding after sex", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "My testicle hurts and feels swollen", "frequency": "rare"}], "hpi": {"onset": "Gradual; symptoms, when present, usually begin 1–3 weeks after exposure, but many patients never notice a clear onset", "duration": "Days to weeks; often present for weeks before the patient seeks care, and frequently detected only on routine screening", "progression": "Most infections stay asymptomatic or produce mild symptoms that wax and wane; untreated infection can ascend over weeks to months and cause pelvic inflammatory disease in women or epididymitis in men", "location": "Urethra, cervix, rectum, or pharynx depending on exposure; pelvic or lower abdominal pain if infection ascends", "radiation": "Pelvic pain may radiate to the lower back or thighs; testicular pain may radiate along the spermatic cord", "quality": "Burning or stinging with urination; thin discharge; dull pelvic or scrotal ache", "severity": "Usually mild; pelvic inflammatory disease and epididymitis are more painful and can be moderate to severe", "timing": "Dysuria is constant with urination; discharge may be noticed most in the morning; pelvic pain is often constant or worse with movement and intercourse", "aggravating": ["Urination", "Sexual intercourse", "Walking or jarring movements (with pelvic involvement)"], "relieving": ["Nothing specific", "Rest (with pelvic pain)"], "associated_symptoms": [{"text": "No symptoms at all", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Vaginal or penile discharge", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Burning or pain with urination", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Vaginal bleeding between periods or after sex", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Lower abdominal or pelvic pain", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Pain with sex", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Rectal pain, discharge, or bleeding with rectal infection", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Sore throat with pharyngeal infection", "frequency": "rare"}, {"text": "Painful, swollen testicle", "frequency": "rare"}, {"text": "Fever", "frequency": "rare"}], "pertinent_negatives": ["Profuse, frothy, malodorous discharge", "Genital ulcers or sores", "Marked fever and rigors at first presentation", "Severe unilateral testicular pain of sudden onset with nausea and vomiting", "Missed period with one-sided pelvic pain and vaginal bleeding", "Joint swelling and rash preceding the genital symptoms"]}, "ros": [{"system": "constitutional", "positives": [{"text": "Feeling tired", "frequency": "sometimes"}], "negatives": ["Fever", "Chills", "Weight loss", "Night sweats"]}, {"system": "gastrointestinal", "positives": [{"text": "Lower abdominal discomfort", "frequency": "sometimes"}], "negatives": ["Diarrhea", "Blood in stool", "Vomiting"], "argues_against": ["Acute gastroenteritis", "Appendicitis with migration of pain"]}, {"system": "genitourinary", "positives": [{"text": "Burning with urination", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Urethral or vaginal discharge", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Urinary frequency", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Rectal pain or discharge", "frequency": "sometimes"}], "negatives": ["Blood in urine", "Flank pain", "Passing a kidney stone"], "argues_against": ["Pyelonephritis", "Nephrolithiasis"]}, {"system": "reproductive", "positives": [{"text": "Vaginal discharge", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Bleeding after sex", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Pain with sex", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Testicular pain or swelling", "frequency": "rare"}], "negatives": ["Genital ulcers", "Missed period", "Breast tenderness"], "argues_against": ["Herpes simplex genital infection", "Ectopic pregnancy", "Primary syphilis"]}, {"system": "musculoskeletal", "positives": [], "negatives": ["Joint swelling", "Morning stiffness", "Low back pain"], "argues_against": ["Reactive arthritis"]}, {"system": "skin", "positives": [], "negatives": ["Rash", "Sores on the palms or soles"], "argues_against": ["Secondary syphilis"]}], "atypical": [{"population": "pregnancy", "how_it_differs": "Usually asymptomatic and found on routine prenatal screening; untreated infection is linked to preterm birth, low birth weight, and neonatal conjunctivitis or pneumonia, so screening at the first prenatal visit is standard.", "missing_classic_features": ["Dysuria", "Discharge noticed by the patient"]}, {"population": "children", "how_it_differs": "Genital chlamydia in a prepubertal child raises concern for sexual abuse; infection can also present as neonatal conjunctivitis or afebrile pneumonia in infants born to infected mothers.", "missing_classic_features": ["Sexual history", "Dysuria described by the patient"]}, {"population": "immunocompromised", "how_it_differs": "People with HIV or other immunosuppression may have more persistent or severe symptoms and higher rates of repeat infection, and co-infection with gonorrhea is more likely.", "missing_classic_features": ["Mild, self-limited course"]}, {"population": "older_adults", "how_it_differs": "Less often considered, so diagnosis is delayed; presentation is the same but clinicians may attribute discharge or dysuria to urinary tract infection or atrophy instead.", "missing_classic_features": ["Being in the usual screening age group"]}]}, "exam": {"general": {"mild": "Well-appearing, comfortable, no distress", "moderate": "Mildly uncomfortable, may shift in the chair with pelvic or scrotal pain", "severe": "Ill-appearing, guarding the lower abdomen, reluctant to move", "critical": "Toxic-appearing, febrile, tachycardic, diaphoretic"}, "findings": [{"id": "mucopurulent-cervical-discharge", "region": "pelvic", "maneuver": "Insert a speculum and inspect the cervix", "finding": "Mucopurulent cervical discharge", "frequency": "common", "significance": "strongly_supports", "importance": "essential"}, {"id": "cervical-motion-tenderness", "region": "pelvic", "maneuver": "Move the cervix side to side with the examining fingers", "finding": "Cervical motion tenderness", "frequency": "sometimes", "min_severity": "moderate", "significance": "strongly_supports", "importance": "essential"}, {"id": "friable-cervix", "region": "pelvic", "maneuver": "Swab the cervix or observe after speculum insertion", "finding": "Easy bleeding from the cervix (friability)", "frequency": "sometimes", "significance": "supports", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "adnexal-tenderness", "region": "pelvic", "maneuver": "Palpate the adnexa bimanually", "finding": "Adnexal tenderness", "frequency": "sometimes", "min_severity": "moderate", "significance": "supports", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "adnexal-mass", "region": "pelvic", "maneuver": "Palpate the adnexa bimanually", "finding": "Adnexal mass or fullness (tubo-ovarian abscess)", "frequency": "rare", "min_severity": "severe", "significance": "supports", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "urethral-discharge", "region": "genitourinary", "maneuver": "Inspect the urethral meatus and strip the urethra", "finding": "Clear or mucopurulent urethral discharge", "frequency": "common", "significance": "strongly_supports", "importance": "essential"}, {"id": "testicular-tenderness", "region": "genitourinary", "maneuver": "Palpate the testis and epididymis", "finding": "Tenderness and induration of the epididymis, usually unilateral", "frequency": "rare", "min_severity": "moderate", "significance": "supports", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "testicular-swelling", "region": "genitourinary", "maneuver": "Inspect and palpate the scrotum", "finding": "Scrotal swelling or hydrocele", "frequency": "rare", "min_severity": "moderate", "significance": "supports", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "prehn-sign", "region": "genitourinary", "maneuver": "Elevate the affected testis", "finding": "Relief of pain with elevation (suggests epididymitis rather than torsion)", "frequency": "sometimes", "min_severity": "moderate", "significance": "supports", "importance": "optional"}, {"id": "cremasteric-reflex", "region": "genitourinary", "maneuver": "Stroke the inner thigh and observe the scrotum", "finding": "Cremasteric reflex present (argues against testicular torsion)", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "argues_against", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "lower-abdominal-tenderness", "region": "abdomen", "maneuver": "Palpate the lower abdomen", "finding": "Suprapubic or lower abdominal tenderness", "frequency": "sometimes", "min_severity": "moderate", "significance": "supports", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "rebound-tenderness", "region": "abdomen", "maneuver": "Assess for rebound tenderness or ask the patient to cough", "finding": "Rebound tenderness suggesting peritoneal irritation", "frequency": "rare", "min_severity": "severe", "significance": "supports", "importance": "essential"}, {"id": "no-flank-tenderness", "region": "abdomen", "maneuver": "Percuss the costovertebral angles", "finding": "No costovertebral angle tenderness", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "neutral", "importance": "optional"}, {"id": "no-oral-lesions", "region": "heent", "maneuver": "Inspect the oral cavity and pharynx", "finding": "No ulcers, exudate, or erythema", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "neutral", "importance": "optional"}, {"id": "no-inguinal-lymphadenopathy", "region": "lymph", "maneuver": "Palpate the inguinal lymph nodes", "finding": "No inguinal lymphadenopathy", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "neutral", "importance": "optional"}, {"id": "no-genital-ulcers", "region": "genitourinary", "maneuver": "Inspect the external genitalia", "finding": "No ulcers, vesicles, or erosions", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "neutral", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "no-rash", "region": "skin", "maneuver": "Inspect the skin, including palms and soles", "finding": "No rash or mucocutaneous lesions", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "neutral", "importance": "optional"}, {"id": "no-joint-swelling", "region": "musculoskeletal", "maneuver": "Inspect and palpate the joints", "finding": "No joint swelling, warmth, or tenderness", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "neutral", "importance": "optional"}]}, "vitals": {"temp": {"moderate": {"min": 99.5, "max": 100.9}, "severe": {"min": 100.4, "max": 102.2}, "critical": {"min": 101.0, "max": 103.5}}, "hr": {"moderate": {"min": 88, "max": 108}, "severe": {"min": 100, "max": 124}, "critical": {"min": 115, "max": 145}}, "rr": {"severe": {"min": 18, "max": 24}, "critical": {"min": 22, "max": 30}}, "sbp": {"critical": {"min": 78, "max": 98}}, "dbp": {"critical": {"min": 40, "max": 60}}}, "labs": [{"test_id": "ua_le", "qualitative": {"moderate": "1+", "severe": "2+"}, "frequency": "common", "importance": "important", "caveat": "Sterile pyuria — leukocyte esterase may be positive while the urine culture is negative; this is a common clue to urethritis rather than a urinary tract infection."}, {"test_id": "ua_wbc", "ranges": {"moderate": {"min": 6, "max": 20}, "severe": {"min": 10, "max": 40}}, "frequency": "common", "importance": "important", "caveat": "White cells in urine without bacteriuria supports urethritis or cervicitis."}, {"test_id": "ua_nitrite", "qualitative": {"moderate": "Positive"}, "frequency": "rare", "importance": "optional", "caveat": "A positive nitrite points toward a true urinary tract infection rather than chlamydial urethritis."}, {"test_id": "ua_bacteria", "qualitative": {"moderate": "Few"}, "frequency": "rare", "importance": "optional"}, {"test_id": "wbc", "ranges": {"moderate": {"min": 11.0, "max": 15.0}, "severe": {"min": 14.0, "max": 20.0}}, "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "important", "caveat": "Usually normal in uncomplicated urethritis or cervicitis; a rise suggests ascending infection such as pelvic inflammatory disease or epididymitis."}, {"test_id": "neut_pct", "ranges": {"moderate": {"min": 72, "max": 85}, "severe": {"min": 78, "max": 90}}, "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "optional"}, {"test_id": "crp", "ranges": {"moderate": {"min": 1.5, "max": 6.0}, "severe": {"min": 4.0, "max": 15.0}}, "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "optional", "caveat": "Nonspecific; may be mildly elevated with pelvic inflammatory disease."}, {"test_id": "esr", "ranges": {"moderate": {"min": 25, "max": 50}, "severe": {"min": 40, "max": 80}}, "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "optional"}, {"test_id": "hcg", "frequency": "almost_always", "importance": "essential", "caveat": "Negative. Required in anyone who could be pregnant before imaging or treatment; also rules out ectopic pregnancy when pelvic pain is present."}], "imaging": [{"study": "Pelvic ultrasound (transvaginal or transabdominal)", "modality": "ultrasound", "preferred_when": "Pelvic pain, suspected pelvic inflammatory disease, or possible pregnancy", "findings": [{"text": "Normal ovaries and tubes in uncomplicated cervicitis or urethritis", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Thickened, fluid-filled fallopian tubes (pyosalpinx) with pelvic inflammatory disease", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Free pelvic fluid", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Tubo-ovarian complex or abscess", "frequency": "rare"}]}, {"study": "Scrotal ultrasound with Doppler", "modality": "ultrasound", "preferred_when": "Testicular pain or swelling when torsion is a concern", "findings": [{"text": "Enlarged, hyperemic epididymis with preserved or increased testicular flow (epididymitis)", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Reactive hydrocele", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Absent or reduced testicular flow (testicular torsion — a must-not-miss alternative)", "frequency": "rare"}]}, {"study": "CT abdomen and pelvis with IV contrast", "modality": "ct", "findings": [{"text": "Usually normal; not needed for uncomplicated genital chlamydia", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Tubal thickening, pelvic fluid, or tubo-ovarian abscess when infection has ascended", "frequency": "rare"}]}], "workup": {"initial": [{"test": "hcg", "why": "Rules out pregnancy and ectopic pregnancy before imaging or antibiotics"}, {"test": "ua", "why": "Sterile pyuria supports urethritis and helps exclude a urinary tract infection"}, {"test": "Pelvic ultrasound (transvaginal or transabdominal)", "why": "Looks for tubo-ovarian abscess or free fluid", "only_if": "pelvic pain, adnexal tenderness, or suspected pelvic inflammatory disease"}, {"test": "Scrotal ultrasound with Doppler", "why": "Distinguishes epididymitis from testicular torsion", "only_if": "testicular pain or swelling"}], "confirmatory": [{"test": "Nucleic acid amplification test (NAAT) for Chlamydia trachomatis on first-catch urine", "why": "Preferred noninvasive test in males and in patients who decline a swab"}, {"test": "Nucleic acid amplification test (NAAT) for Chlamydia trachomatis on a vaginal or endocervical swab", "why": "Preferred specimen in females; self-collected vaginal swabs are acceptable"}, {"test": "Nucleic acid amplification test (NAAT) for Chlamydia trachomatis on a rectal swab", "why": "Detects rectal infection in patients reporting anal sex", "only_if": "anal exposure or rectal symptoms"}, {"test": "Nucleic acid amplification test (NAAT) for Chlamydia trachomatis on a pharyngeal swab", "why": "Detects pharyngeal infection in patients reporting oral sex", "only_if": "oral exposure or sore throat"}], "supporting": [{"test": "Nucleic acid amplification test (NAAT) for Neisseria gonorrhoeae", "why": "Co-infection is common and changes treatment"}, {"test": "HIV test", "why": "Recommended for anyone diagnosed with an STI"}, {"test": "Rapid plasma reagin (RPR) or treponemal test for syphilis", "why": "Recommended STI screening at the same visit"}, {"test": "Hepatitis B surface antigen and hepatitis C antibody", "why": "STI screening in patients with a new diagnosis"}, {"test": "Wet mount or vaginal pH", "why": "Evaluates concurrent bacterial vaginosis or trichomoniasis", "only_if": "vaginal discharge or odor"}, {"test": "Urine culture", "why": "Excludes a true urinary tract infection when pyuria is present", "only_if": "dysuria with positive leukocyte esterase or nitrite"}], "not_indicated": [{"test": "CT abdomen and pelvis with IV contrast", "why": "Rarely changes management of uncomplicated chlamydia; reserve for suspected abscess or an unclear surgical abdomen"}, {"test": "Pelvic ultrasound (transvaginal or transabdominal)", "why": "Not needed when there is no pelvic pain or adnexal tenderness"}, {"test": "Scrotal ultrasound with Doppler", "why": "Not needed without testicular pain or swelling"}, {"test": "crp", "why": "Nonspecific and does not change management"}, {"test": "esr", "why": "Nonspecific and does not change management"}, {"test": "cbc", "why": "Usually normal in uncomplicated infection"}, {"test": "bmp", "why": "No role unless the patient is dehydrated or systemically ill"}, {"test": "d_dimer", "why": "No role in pelvic or scrotal pain from an STI"}], "decision_points": ["Pregnancy test before imaging or antibiotics in anyone who could be pregnant", "NAAT is the test of choice; culture is reserved for rare situations such as suspected treatment failure or medico-legal cases", "Test all exposed sites (urine or vaginal/cervical, rectal, pharyngeal) based on reported sexual practices", "Treat empirically for pelvic inflammatory disease when cervical motion tenderness, adnexal tenderness, or uterine tenderness is present, without waiting for NAAT results", "Test and treat partners; patients should abstain from sex until 7 days after single-dose therapy or completion of a 7-day regimen and until partners are treated", "Repeat testing about 3 months after treatment because reinfection is common", "Sudden severe unilateral testicular pain with an absent cremasteric reflex is torsion until proven otherwise — do not wait for NAAT"], "criteria": [], "scores": []}, "differential": [{"name": "Gonorrhea", "slug": "gonorrhea", "why_considered": "Nearly identical presentation with urethral or cervical discharge and dysuria; co-infection is common.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Purulent, copious discharge or known partner with gonorrhea", "discriminators": {"history": ["More abrupt onset with heavier, thicker discharge", "Higher likelihood of symptomatic presentation in men"], "exam": ["Profuse purulent urethral or cervical discharge", "May have more marked pelvic tenderness if ascending"], "labs": ["NAAT positive for Neisseria gonorrhoeae", "Gram stain of urethral discharge showing gram-negative intracellular diplococci in men"]}}, {"name": "Trichomoniasis", "slug": "trichomoniasis", "why_considered": "Vaginal discharge and dysuria in a sexually active patient.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Frothy, malodorous yellow-green discharge", "Vulvar itching and irritation"], "exam": ["Strawberry cervix (punctate hemorrhages)", "Vaginal pH greater than 4.5"], "labs": ["Motile trichomonads on wet mount", "Positive NAAT for Trichomonas vaginalis"]}}, {"name": "Bacterial vaginosis", "slug": "bacterial-vaginosis", "why_considered": "Vaginal discharge and odor without prominent dysuria.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Thin gray discharge with a fishy odor, worse after intercourse", "Minimal or no dysuria"], "exam": ["Homogeneous discharge coating the vaginal walls", "No cervical motion tenderness"], "labs": ["Vaginal pH greater than 4.5", "Clue cells on wet mount", "Positive whiff test"]}}, {"name": "Herpes simplex genital infection", "slug": "genital-herpes", "why_considered": "Genital pain, dysuria, and discharge in a sexually active patient.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Prodrome of tingling or burning before lesions", "Severe dysuria and external pain"], "exam": ["Multiple painful vesicles or ulcers on the genitalia", "Inguinal lymphadenopathy"], "labs": ["Positive HSV PCR from a lesion swab", "Tzanck smear showing multinucleated giant cells"]}}, {"name": "Urinary tract infection", "slug": "urinary-tract-infection", "why_considered": "Dysuria and urinary frequency.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["No new sexual partner or discharge", "Suprapubic pain and urgency"], "exam": ["Suprapubic tenderness without cervical motion tenderness", "No urethral or cervical discharge"], "labs": ["Positive urine nitrite and leukocyte esterase", "Urine culture growing a uropathogen"]}}, {"name": "Ectopic pregnancy", "slug": "ectopic-pregnancy", "why_considered": "Lower abdominal or pelvic pain in a patient who could be pregnant.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Any patient with pelvic pain who could be pregnant", "discriminators": {"history": ["Missed period", "Vaginal bleeding", "Unilateral pelvic pain"], "exam": ["Adnexal tenderness or mass", "Peritoneal signs if ruptured"], "labs": ["Positive hCG", "Falling hematocrit if ruptured"], "imaging": ["No intrauterine pregnancy on transvaginal ultrasound", "Free fluid in the pelvis"]}}, {"name": "Testicular torsion", "slug": "testicular-torsion", "why_considered": "Acute testicular pain and swelling.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Sudden severe unilateral testicular pain with nausea or vomiting", "discriminators": {"history": ["Abrupt onset of severe pain", "Nausea and vomiting at onset", "No dysuria or discharge"], "exam": ["Absent cremasteric reflex", "High-riding testis with transverse lie"], "imaging": ["Absent or reduced testicular blood flow on Doppler ultrasound"]}}, {"name": "Reactive arthritis", "slug": "reactive-arthritis", "why_considered": "Joint complaints in a patient with a recent STI.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Joint pain and swelling weeks after urethritis or cervicitis", "Eye redness or pain"], "exam": ["Joint swelling, warmth, and tenderness", "Conjunctivitis or uveitis"], "labs": ["Elevated ESR and CRP", "Negative rheumatoid factor and anti-CCP"]}}], "course": {"severity": {"mild": "Asymptomatic or minimal symptoms; detected on routine screening or partner notification; no systemic signs.", "moderate": "Symptomatic urethritis or cervicitis with discharge, dysuria, or postcoital bleeding; no fever or peritoneal signs.", "severe": "Ascending infection such as pelvic inflammatory disease or epididymitis with fever, pelvic or scrotal pain, and leukocytosis.", "critical": "Ruptured tubo-ovarian abscess with generalized peritonitis and sepsis; hypotension, tachycardia, and diffuse abdominal rigidity."}, "red_flags": [{"finding": "Sudden severe unilateral testicular pain with absent cremasteric reflex", "meaning": "Testicular torsion until proven otherwise; requires immediate urologic evaluation"}, {"finding": "Positive pregnancy test with pelvic pain or bleeding", "meaning": "Ectopic pregnancy must be excluded"}, {"finding": "Diffuse abdominal rigidity or rebound tenderness", "meaning": "Peritonitis, possibly from a ruptured tubo-ovarian abscess"}, {"finding": "Hypotension or tachycardia", "meaning": "Sepsis; needs urgent resuscitation and broad-spectrum antibiotics"}, {"finding": "High fever with severe pelvic pain", "meaning": "Tubo-ovarian abscess or severe pelvic inflammatory disease"}], "complications": [{"name": "Pelvic inflammatory disease", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Lower abdominal pain", "Cervical motion tenderness", "Fever", "Mucopurulent cervical discharge"]}, {"name": "Tubo-ovarian abscess", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Persistent fever", "Palpable adnexal mass", "Pelvic pain unresponsive to oral antibiotics"]}, {"name": "Infertility", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["History of recurrent or untreated chlamydia", "Prior pelvic inflammatory disease"]}, {"name": "Ectopic pregnancy", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["History of pelvic inflammatory disease", "Missed period with pelvic pain and bleeding"]}, {"name": "Epididymitis", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Unilateral testicular pain and swelling", "Tenderness of the epididymis", "Relief with testicular elevation"]}, {"name": "Reactive arthritis", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Joint pain and swelling", "Conjunctivitis", "Urethritis"]}, {"name": "Neonatal conjunctivitis", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Eye discharge and redness in an infant born to an infected mother", "Onset 5–14 days after birth"]}, {"name": "Neonatal pneumonia", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Afebrile pneumonia in an infant 2–12 weeks old", "Tachypnea and cough"]}], "special_populations": [{"population": "pregnancy", "differences": "Usually asymptomatic and found on routine prenatal screening; untreated infection is linked to preterm birth, low birth weight, and neonatal conjunctivitis or pneumonia. Screening at the first prenatal visit is standard, and treatment with azithromycin or amoxicillin is recommended."}, {"population": "children", "differences": "Genital chlamydia in a prepubertal child raises concern for sexual abuse; infection can also present as neonatal conjunctivitis or afebrile pneumonia in infants born to infected mothers."}, {"population": "immunocompromised", "differences": "People with HIV or other immunosuppression may have more persistent or severe symptoms and higher rates of repeat infection, and co-infection with gonorrhea is more likely."}, {"population": "older_adults", "differences": "Less often considered, so diagnosis is delayed; presentation is the same but clinicians may attribute discharge or dysuria to urinary tract infection or atrophy instead."}]}, "teaching": {"key_questions": [{"question": "Do you have any symptoms, or are you here because a partner tested positive or because of routine screening?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Many patients are asymptomatic; partner notification or screening is a common reason for presentation"}, {"question": "What symptoms have you noticed — discharge, burning with urination, pelvic or belly pain, bleeding between periods or after sex?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Thin discharge, dysuria, postcoital or intermenstrual bleeding, or pelvic pain"}, {"question": "When did your symptoms start?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Gradual onset about 1–3 weeks after exposure; often present for weeks before care is sought"}, {"question": "Have you had a new sex partner or more than one partner in the past year?", "category": "sexual", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "New or multiple partners raises pretest probability and guides testing"}, {"question": "Do you use condoms? How consistently?", "category": "sexual", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Inconsistent or no condom use"}, {"question": "Do you have sex with men, women, or both? Do you have vaginal, anal, or oral sex?", "category": "sexual", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Determines which sites to test — urine or vaginal/cervical, rectal, and pharyngeal"}, {"question": "Has a current or past partner been diagnosed with an STI, or had discharge or burning with urination?", "category": "sexual", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Partner with a known STI or urethral symptoms strongly supports the diagnosis"}, {"question": "Have you ever had an STI before, including chlamydia or gonorrhea?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Prior STI increases risk of reinfection and complications"}, {"question": "Could you be pregnant? When was your first day of your last period?", "category": "obstetric", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Pregnancy changes treatment choices and, with pelvic pain, ectopic pregnancy must be excluded"}, {"question": "Do you have any lower belly pain, pain with sex, or fever?", "category": "associated", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Suggests ascending infection such as pelvic inflammatory disease"}, {"question": "Any pain or swelling in one testicle?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Epididymitis; sudden severe pain suggests torsion instead"}, {"question": "Any rectal pain, discharge, or bleeding? Any sore throat?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Rectal or pharyngeal infection based on exposure"}, {"question": "Any joint pain or swelling, eye redness, or rash?", "category": "ros", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Reactive arthritis or another STI such as secondary syphilis"}, {"question": "Are you taking any antibiotics or other medications right now?", "category": "medications", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Recent antibiotics may affect test results; drug interactions matter for treatment choice"}, {"question": "Do you have any medication allergies, especially to antibiotics?", "category": "allergies", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Penicillin or cephalosporin allergy changes the regimen"}, {"question": "Do you have any concerns about safety at home, or has anyone pressured you into sex?", "category": "social", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Intimate partner violence or coercion, which changes counseling and follow-up"}], "accepted_names": ["Chlamydia", "Chlamydia infection", "Chlamydial infection", "Chlamydia trachomatis infection", "CT infection", "Chlamydial urethritis", "Chlamydial cervicitis"], "reasoning_points": ["Sexual history identifies a new or multiple partners, inconsistent condom use, or a partner with a known STI", "Symptoms of urethritis or cervicitis — discharge, dysuria, postcoital bleeding — or the patient may be entirely asymptomatic", "Onset about 1–3 weeks after exposure fits the incubation period", "Exam may show mucopurulent cervical discharge, a friable cervix, or urethral discharge; cervical motion tenderness suggests pelvic inflammatory disease", "Sterile pyuria on urinalysis supports urethritis rather than a urinary tract infection", "Nucleic acid amplification testing of the appropriate sites confirms the diagnosis", "Gonorrhea co-infection, HIV, and syphilis should be tested at the same visit", "Pregnancy must be excluded before treatment and because of the risk of ectopic pregnancy with pelvic pain", "Partners must be treated and the patient should abstain from sex until therapy is completed"], "difficulty": {"easy": ["Sexually active young adult with a new partner and no symptoms, presenting after a partner tested positive", "Thin urethral discharge with dysuria in a young man", "Mucopurulent cervical discharge with postcoital bleeding in a young woman"], "medium": ["Asymptomatic patient detected on routine screening with no complaints at all", "Dysuria with sterile pyuria and a negative urine culture", "Rectal infection presenting with rectal pain or discharge", "Patient with concurrent gonorrhea and chlamydia"], "hard": ["Pelvic inflammatory disease with fever and cervical motion tenderness where chlamydia is the underlying cause", "Epididymitis with unilateral testicular pain where torsion must first be excluded", "Pregnant patient found on routine prenatal screening with no symptoms", "Older adult whose discharge or dysuria is mistakenly attributed to urinary tract infection or atrophy", "Prepubertal child with genital chlamydia raising concern for sexual abuse"]}}}$rounds_kb$::jsonb)
