al arteries", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Asymmetric kidney size", "frequency": "sometimes"}]}, {"study": "Echocardiogram", "modality": "echo", "preferred_when": "Suspected heart failure, valvular disease, or pericardial effusion", "findings": [{"text": "Left ventricular hypertrophy", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Diastolic dysfunction", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Reduced ejection fraction", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Pericardial effusion", "frequency": "rare"}]}, {"study": "Chest x-ray", "modality": "xray", "preferred_when": "Shortness of breath or suspected volume overload", "findings": [{"text": "Pulmonary vascular congestion or edema", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Small bilateral pleural effusions", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Enlarged cardiac silhouette", "frequency": "sometimes"}]}], "workup": {"initial": [{"test": "bmp", "why": "Measures creatinine, potassium, bicarbonate, and calcium; eGFR is calculated from creatinine to stage CKD"}, {"test": "ua", "why": "Looks for protein, blood, cells, and casts that point to a glomerular or interstitial cause"}, {"test": "cbc", "why": "Detects anemia of CKD and other blood abnormalities"}, {"test": "a1c", "why": "Identifies diabetes as the cause or a comorbidity"}, {"test": "lipids", "why": "Cardiovascular risk assessment and statin decisions"}, {"test": "Renal ultrasound", "why": "Assesses kidney size, symmetry, cysts, and obstruction"}], "confirmatory": [{"test": "Urine protein-to-creatinine ratio", "why": "Quantifies proteinuria, which confirms kidney damage and predicts progression"}, {"test": "Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio", "why": "Detects albuminuria, a key marker of kidney damage and cardiovascular risk"}], "supporting": [{"test": "phos", "why": "Evaluates mineral and bone disorder of CKD"}, {"test": "ferritin", "why": "Evaluates iron stores before treating anemia"}, {"test": "iron", "why": "Evaluates iron stores before treating anemia"}, {"test": "tibc", "why": "Evaluates iron stores before treating anemia"}, {"test": "tsh", "why": "Rules out hypothyroidism when fatigue or edema is unexplained"}, {"test": "bnp", "why": "Helps assess volume overload or heart failure", "only_if": "shortness of breath or suspected heart failure"}, {"test": "nt_probnp", "why": "Helps assess volume overload or heart failure", "only_if": "shortness of breath or suspected heart failure"}, {"test": "Echocardiogram", "why": "Evaluates cardiac structure and function", "only_if": "suspected heart failure, murmur, or pericardial disease"}, {"test": "Chest x-ray", "why": "Assesses pulmonary edema or effusions", "only_if": "shortness of breath or suspected volume overload"}, {"test": "Magnetic resonance angiography of the renal arteries", "why": "Looks for renal artery stenosis", "only_if": "resistant hypertension, flash pulmonary edema, or asymmetric kidneys"}], "not_indicated": [{"test": "CT abdomen and pelvis without contrast", "why": "Not needed for routine CKD evaluation; reserve for suspected stones, mass, or nondiagnostic ultrasound"}, {"test": "Magnetic resonance angiography of the renal arteries", "why": "Not routine; gadolinium risk in advanced CKD"}, {"test": "Echocardiogram", "why": "Not routine unless cardiac disease is suspected"}, {"test": "Chest x-ray", "why": "Not routine unless respiratory symptoms or volume overload are present"}, {"test": "d_dimer", "why": "No role in CKD evaluation"}, {"test": "lipase", "why": "Not indicated unless pancreatitis is suspected"}, {"test": "lactate", "why": "Not indicated unless sepsis or hypoperfusion is suspected"}, {"test": "procalcitonin", "why": "Not indicated unless bacterial infection is suspected"}], "decision_points": ["Confirm chronicity: kidney damage or reduced eGFR must persist for more than 3 months", "Use eGFR to assign CKD stage; use albuminuria category to refine risk", "Rule out reversible causes such as obstruction, nephrotoxic drugs, and volume depletion before labeling as CKD", "Avoid NSAIDs and adjust doses of renally cleared medications", "Refer to nephrology for eGFR below 30, rapidly declining function, difficult hypertension, or persistent hyperkalemia"], "criteria": [{"name": "CKD definition and staging", "summary": "CKD is present when there is evidence of kidney damage (such as albuminuria, urine sediment abnormalities, or structural changes) or an eGFR below 60 mL/min/1.73 m² for at least 3 months. Stages are based on eGFR: G1 ≥90, G2 60–89, G3a 45–59, G3b 30–44, G4 15–29, G5 <15 mL/min/1.73 m². Albuminuria categories are A1 <30 mg/g, A2 30–300 mg/g, A3 >300 mg/g."}], "scores": []}, "differential": [{"name": "Acute kidney injury", "slug": "acute-kidney-injury", "why_considered": "Rising creatinine and reduced urine output look identical on a single set of labs", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Creatinine that has risen over days to weeks rather than months to years, or a clear precipitant such as dehydration, contrast, or a new nephrotoxic drug", "discriminators": {"history": ["Onset over hours to days", "Recent vomiting, diarrhea, poor intake, or bleeding", "New NSAID, contrast dye, or other nephrotoxic exposure", "Recent hospitalization or surgery"], "exam": ["Signs of volume depletion such as dry mucous membranes, low jugular venous pressure, or orthostatic changes", "Normal or large kidneys rather than small ones"], "labs": ["Creatinine rising quickly between two draws", "Fractional excretion of sodium or urea can help separate prerenal from intrinsic causes", "FeNa below 1% suggests a prerenal picture", "Muddy brown granular casts suggest acute tubular necrosis"], "imaging": ["Renal ultrasound shows normal-sized or enlarged kidneys without cortical thinning"]}}, {"name": "Nephrotic syndrome", "slug": "nephrotic-syndrome", "why_considered": "Heavy proteinuria, low albumin, and edema overlap with advanced CKD", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Foamy urine with marked proteinuria, low serum albumin, and periorbital or generalized edema out of proportion to the creatinine", "discriminators": {"history": ["Abrupt or subacute onset of swelling", "Foamy urine", "Recent infection or new medication", "History of a systemic disease such as lupus or diabetes"], "exam": ["Marked periorbital and generalized edema", "Normal or only mildly elevated blood pressure", "Possible thrombosis or infection"], "labs": ["Urine protein-to-creatinine ratio in the nephrotic range", "Serum albumin markedly low", "Creatinine may be normal or only mildly elevated", "Lipid panel often shows high cholesterol"], "imaging": ["Renal ultrasound may show normal or enlarged kidneys"]}}, {"name": "Heart failure", "slug": "heart-failure", "why_considered": "Fatigue, leg swelling, and shortness of breath are shared with CKD, and the two often coexist", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Orthopnea, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea, or an S3 gallop with elevated jugular venous pressure", "discriminators": {"history": ["Waking at night short of breath", "Needing extra pillows to sleep", "Known coronary disease or prior myocardial infarction"], "exam": ["Elevated jugular venous pressure", "S3 gallop", "Bibasilar crackles", "Cardiomegaly"], "labs": ["Elevated BNP or NT-proBNP", "Creatinine may be normal or only mildly elevated"], "imaging": ["Echocardiogram shows reduced ejection fraction or diastolic dysfunction", "Chest x-ray shows pulmonary congestion or cardiomegaly"]}}, {"name": "Hypothyroidism", "slug": "hypothyroidism", "why_considered": "Fatigue, cold intolerance, edema, and constipation overlap with CKD", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Weight gain despite poor appetite", "Cold intolerance", "Constipation", "Dry skin and hair loss"], "exam": ["Diffuse nonpitting edema (myxedema)", "Delayed relaxation of deep tendon reflexes", "Bradycardia"], "labs": ["Elevated TSH with low free T4", "Creatinine usually normal or only mildly elevated"]}}, {"name": "Anemia of chronic disease", "slug": "anemia-of-chronic-disease", "why_considered": "Normocytic anemia and fatigue are shared with CKD", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Underlying inflammatory condition such as rheumatoid arthritis or chronic infection", "Nocturia and foamy urine are absent"], "exam": ["Pallor without edema or hypertension"], "labs": ["Low hemoglobin with normal creatinine and eGFR", "Low iron with low transferrin saturation but normal or high ferritin", "Urinalysis without protein or blood"]}}, {"name": "Multiple myeloma", "slug": "multiple-myeloma", "why_considered": "Anemia, fatigue, and elevated creatinine from light chain kidney injury can mimic CKD", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Anemia with bone pain, hypercalcemia, or a high total protein with a low albumin gap", "discriminators": {"history": ["Bone pain, especially back or ribs", "Recurrent infections", "Weight loss"], "exam": ["Pallor", "Bone tenderness", "No edema"], "labs": ["Elevated total protein with low albumin", "Hypercalcemia", "Proteinuria that is not primarily albumin on urine protein electrophoresis", "Serum free light chain ratio abnormal"], "imaging": ["Skeletal survey or whole-body low-dose CT shows lytic lesions"]}}, {"name": "Renal artery stenosis", "slug": "renal-artery-stenosis", "why_considered": "Progressive kidney dysfunction with difficult-to-control hypertension", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Resistant hypertension on three or more agents", "Flash pulmonary edema", "Onset of hypertension after age 55 or before age 30", "Smoking history"], "exam": ["Abdominal bruit over the epigastrium or flank", "Asymmetric kidney size on imaging"], "labs": ["Creatinine rises after starting an ACE inhibitor or ARB", "Hypokalemia from secondary hyperaldosteronism"], "imaging": ["Magnetic resonance angiography or CT angiography shows narrowing at the origin of a renal artery", "Asymmetric kidney size on ultrasound"]}}, {"name": "Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease", "slug": "chronic-obstructive-pulmonary-disease", "why_considered": "Fatigue and shortness of breath are shared, and both are common in older smokers", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Chronic productive cough", "Long smoking history", "Wheezing and recurrent respiratory infections"], "exam": ["Barrel chest", "Prolonged expiratory phase", "Wheezing"], "labs": ["Normal creatinine and eGFR", "Elevated bicarbonate from chronic CO2 retention"], "imaging": ["Chest x-ray shows hyperinflation and flattened diaphragms"]}}], "course": {"severity": {"mild": "CKD stage G1–G2 with albuminuria or G3a: eGFR 45–89 mL/min/1.73 m², no symptoms, normal or near-normal exam, detected on routine labs", "moderate": "CKD stage G3b: eGFR 30–44 mL/min/1.73 m², mild fatigue, early anemia, rising phosphate and PTH, mild leg edema, hypertension", "severe": "CKD stage G4: eGFR 15–29 mL/min/1.73 m², marked fatigue, anemia, edema, hyperkalemia, metabolic acidosis, itching, restless legs, early uremic symptoms", "critical": "CKD stage G5 (kidney failure): eGFR below 15 mL/min/1.73 m², uremic symptoms with nausea, vomiting, confusion, asterixis, pericarditis, pulmonary edema, severe hyperkalemia, or refractory acidosis; dialysis or transplant needed"}, "red_flags": [{"finding": "Potassium above 6.0 mmol/L or ECG showing peaked T waves or widened QRS", "meaning": "Life-threatening hyperkalemia; needs urgent treatment"}, {"finding": "Bicarbonate below 15 mmol/L with tachypnea", "meaning": "Severe metabolic acidosis; may need dialysis"}, {"finding": "Confusion, drowsiness, or asterixis", "meaning": "Uremic encephalopathy; urgent dialysis evaluation"}, {"finding": "Pericardial friction rub or new hypotension with low-voltage ECG", "meaning": "Uremic pericarditis or pericardial effusion; urgent evaluation"}, {"finding": "Rapidly rising creatinine over days to weeks", "meaning": "Superimposed acute kidney injury or rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis; needs urgent workup"}, {"finding": "New severe shortness of breath with crackles and hypoxia", "meaning": "Pulmonary edema from volume overload; needs urgent treatment"}, {"finding": "Anuria or complete absence of urine", "meaning": "Possible obstruction or vascular catastrophe; needs urgent imaging"}], "complications": [{"name": "Anemia of CKD", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Fatigue and pallor", "Normocytic hemoglobin low", "Low erythropoietin with low iron stores"]}, {"name": "Hyperkalemia", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Muscle weakness", "Palpitations", "Peaked T waves on ECG", "Use of ACE inhibitors, ARBs, or spironolactone"]}, {"name": "Metabolic acidosis", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Low bicarbonate", "Kussmaul respirations in severe cases", "Muscle wasting and bone loss over time"]}, {"name": "Mineral and bone disorder", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Elevated phosphate and PTH", "Low calcium", "Bone pain and fractures", "Vascular calcification"]}, {"name": "Volume overload and pulmonary edema", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Weight gain", "Leg edema", "Orthopnea", "Crackles and hypoxia"]}, {"name": "Uremic pericarditis", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Pleuritic chest pain worse when lying flat", "Pericardial friction rub", "Low-voltage QRS or electrical alternans on ECG"]}, {"name": "Uremic encephalopathy", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Confusion", "Drowsiness", "Asterixis", "Seizures in advanced cases"]}, {"name": "Peripheral neuropathy", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Numbness and tingling in a stocking distribution", "Reduced vibration sense", "Restless legs"]}, {"name": "Cardiovascular disease", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Left ventricular hypertrophy", "Coronary artery disease", "Heart failure", "Sudden cardiac death"]}, {"name": "Infection risk", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Impaired immune function", "Pneumonia", "Urinary tract infection", "Sepsis"]}, {"name": "Bleeding tendency", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Easy bruising", "Prolonged bleeding time", "Platelet dysfunction from uremia"]}], "special_populations": [{"population": "older_adults", "differences": "Creatinine may look normal despite reduced kidney function because of low muscle mass; use eGFR and cystatin C when available. Symptoms are often attributed to aging, and polypharmacy raises the risk of drug accumulation and nephrotoxicity."}, {"population": "diabetes", "differences": "Diabetes is the most common cause of CKD in the US. Albuminuria often appears before eGFR falls. Tight glucose control and ACE inhibitors or ARBs slow progression. Metformin dose must be adjusted and stopped when eGFR falls below 30 mL/min/1.73 m²."}, {"population": "pregnancy", "differences": "Preeclampsia can mimic or worsen CKD with hypertension, proteinuria, and edema. Creatinine normally falls in pregnancy, so a value in the normal nonpregnant range may indicate kidney dysfunction. CKD raises the risk of preterm delivery and fetal growth restriction."}, {"population": "children", "differences": "CKD in children is usually caused by congenital anomalies or inherited disease rather than diabetes or hypertension. Growth failure and delayed puberty are key concerns, and dosing of all medications must be weight-based."}, {"population": "renal_impairment", "differences": "Patients already on dialysis may have minimal urine output and depend on the machine for fluid and electrolyte balance. Missed dialysis sessions cause rapid volume overload, hyperkalemia, and uremic symptoms."}, {"population": "obesity", "differences": "Obesity increases the risk of CKD and can make blood pressure and edema assessment harder. Weight loss and GLP-1 receptor agonists may slow progression, but dosing and volume status need careful monitoring."}]}, "teaching": {"key_questions": [{"question": "Have you noticed any change in how much you urinate, or are you waking up at night to urinate?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Nocturia and gradual decline in urine output suggest chronic kidney dysfunction rather than an acute process"}, {"question": "How long have you been feeling tired or noticed swelling?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Symptoms present for months to years, supporting chronicity rather than acute kidney injury"}, {"question": "Have you noticed any foamy or bubbly urine?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Foamy urine suggests proteinuria, a marker of kidney damage"}, {"question": "Have you had any blood tests showing kidney problems before this visit?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Prior elevated creatinine or reduced eGFR documented more than 3 months ago confirms chronicity"}, {"question": "Do you have diabetes or high blood pressure?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Diabetes and hypertension are the most common causes of CKD in the US"}, {"question": "Have you ever had a kidney injury, kidney stones, or urinary blockage?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Prior AKI, stones, or obstruction increase risk of CKD"}, {"question": "Do you take any pain relievers like ibuprofen or naproxen regularly?", "category": "medications", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Chronic NSAID use is nephrotoxic and can worsen kidney function"}, {"question": "Are you taking any blood pressure medicines, diabetes medicines, or water pills?", "category": "medications", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "ACE inhibitors, ARBs, diuretics, and metformin require dose adjustment in CKD"}, {"question": "Has anyone in your family had kidney failure or needed dialysis?", "category": "family", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Family history of kidney failure or polycystic kidney disease raises suspicion for inherited causes"}, {"question": "Have you noticed any itching, poor appetite, nausea, or muscle cramps?", "category": "ros", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Uremic symptoms suggest more advanced CKD"}, {"question": "Have you had any chest pain, shortness of breath when lying flat, or swelling in your legs?", "category": "ros", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Volume overload or pericarditis, which can complicate advanced CKD"}, {"question": "Have you ever been told you have anemia or low blood count?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Anemia of CKD from low erythropoietin is common"}, {"question": "Do you smoke or drink alcohol?", "category": "social", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Smoking and alcohol use affect cardiovascular risk and CKD progression"}, {"question": "Have you had any fever, burning with urination, or blood in your urine?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Absence of these argues against acute infection or glomerulonephritis; presence may indicate a different or superimposed process"}], "accepted_names": ["Chronic kidney disease", "CKD", "Chronic renal insufficiency", "Chronic kidney failure", "Chronic renal failure"], "reasoning_points": ["Kidney damage or reduced eGFR persisting for more than 3 months defines chronicity", "eGFR below 60 mL/min/1.73 m² or markers of kidney damage such as albuminuria, urine sediment abnormalities, or structural changes", "Diabetes and hypertension are the most common causes in US adults", "Symptoms are often absent until kidney function is significantly reduced; fatigue, edema, nocturia, and itching are common late findings", "Laboratory findings include elevated creatinine, anemia, hyperkalemia, metabolic acidosis, hyperphosphatemia, and low albumin", "Urinalysis may show proteinuria, hematuria, or casts depending on the underlying cause", "Renal ultrasound typically shows small, echogenic kidneys with cortical thinning", "Staging uses eGFR categories G1–G5 and albuminuria categories A1–A3 to guide management and referral", "Reversible causes such as obstruction, nephrotoxic drugs, and volume depletion must be excluded before labeling as CKD", "Cardiovascular disease is the leading cause of death in CKD; blood pressure and glucose control slow progression"], "difficulty": {"easy": ["Older adult with long-standing diabetes and hypertension", "Incidental elevated creatinine on routine labs", "Classic symptoms of fatigue, leg edema, and nocturia", "Renal ultrasound showing small echogenic kidneys"], "medium": ["Patient with anemia and fatigue but no obvious kidney history", "Normal creatinine in a frail older adult with low muscle mass", "Coexisting heart failure and CKD with overlapping symptoms", "Isolated proteinuria without significant creatinine elevation"], "hard": ["Young patient with CKD from an inherited or congenital cause", "Rapidly progressive course suggesting superimposed acute kidney injury or glomerulonephritis", "Advanced CKD presenting with uremic pericarditis or encephalopathy", "Pregnant patient with hypertension and proteinuria mimicking preeclampsia"]}}}$rounds_kb$::jsonb)
