ties", "Widespread blisters", "Skin peeling"], "argues_against": ["Widespread vesicular rash", "Blisters in a dermatomal pattern"]}, {"system": "eyes", "positives": [], "negatives": ["Eye redness", "Eye pain", "Blurred vision", "Discharge from the eye"], "argues_against": ["Herpetic keratitis"]}, {"system": "neurologic", "positives": [], "negatives": ["Headache", "Confusion", "Neck stiffness", "Seizures"], "argues_against": ["Herpes encephalitis or meningitis"]}, {"system": "gastrointestinal", "positives": [{"text": "Mouth pain that makes eating uncomfortable", "frequency": "sometimes"}], "negatives": ["Vomiting", "Diarrhea", "Abdominal pain"], "argues_against": ["Hand-foot-mouth disease", "Herpangina"]}], "atypical": [{"population": "children", "how_it_differs": "A first infection often causes fever, irritability, swollen gums, many mouth ulcers, drooling, and refusal to eat; the child may not be able to describe tingling.", "missing_classic_features": ["Tingling before the sore", "A single lip sore"]}, {"population": "immunocompromised", "how_it_differs": "Sores can be larger, more numerous, slower to heal, and may spread to other skin areas or inside the mouth.", "missing_classic_features": ["Small, self-limited sore that heals in 1–2 weeks"]}, {"population": "infants", "how_it_differs": "May present with fever, poor feeding, and irritability rather than a clear lip sore; neonatal infection is a serious concern and needs urgent care.", "missing_classic_features": ["Localized lip tingling", "Older child or adult pattern"]}, {"population": "older_adults", "how_it_differs": "May have more frequent or slower-healing outbreaks, and other causes of mouth sores such as medication reactions or nutritional deficiencies are more likely.", "missing_classic_features": ["Typical rapid crusting and healing"]}]}, "exam": {"general": {"mild": "Well-appearing, comfortable, no distress", "moderate": "Well-appearing but uncomfortable when the sore is touched or when eating", "severe": "Ill-appearing with fever and mouth pain, reluctant to eat or drink", "critical": "Toxic-appearing, dehydrated, lethargic (consider disseminated HSV or another diagnosis)"}, "findings": [{"id": "lip-vesicles", "region": "heent", "maneuver": "Inspect the lips, mouth corners, and nostrils in good light", "finding": "Cluster of small, thin-walled, fluid-filled vesicles on an erythematous base at the vermilion border of the lip", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "strongly_supports", "importance": "essential"}, {"id": "lip-crust", "region": "heent", "maneuver": "Inspect the lips and perioral skin", "finding": "Crusted or ulcerated lesion at the lip margin (later stage of the same outbreak)", "frequency": "common", "significance": "strongly_supports", "importance": "essential"}, {"id": "prodromal-erythema", "region": "heent", "maneuver": "Inspect the lip where the patient reports tingling", "finding": "Localized erythema or swelling before vesicles appear", "frequency": "sometimes", "significance": "supports", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "lesion-tenderness", "region": "heent", "maneuver": "Gently palpate the lesion (or ask about tenderness rather than pressing hard)", "finding": "Focal tenderness of the sore", "frequency": "common", "significance": "supports", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "intraoral-ulcers", "region": "heent", "maneuver": "Inspect the gums, buccal mucosa, tongue, and hard palate", "finding": "Multiple shallow ulcers on the gingiva or hard palate (typical of a first episode, primary gingivostomatitis)", "frequency": "sometimes", "significance": "supports", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "gingival-swelling", "region": "heent", "maneuver": "Inspect and gently palpate the gums", "finding": "Swollen, erythematous gingiva that bleeds easily", "frequency": "sometimes", "min_severity": "moderate", "significance": "supports", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "submandibular-nodes", "region": "lymph", "maneuver": "Palpate the submandibular and cervical lymph node chains", "finding": "Tender, enlarged submandibular or anterior cervical lymph nodes", "frequency": "sometimes", "significance": "supports", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "no-eye-involvement", "region": "heent", "maneuver": "Inspect the conjunctivae and ask about eye pain, redness, or blurred vision", "finding": "No conjunctival injection, no corneal lesion, no eye pain or discharge", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "neutral", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "no-neuro-findings", "region": "neuro", "maneuver": "Assess mental status and, if symptoms suggest it, check for neck stiffness and focal deficits", "finding": "Alert and oriented, no neck stiffness, no focal neurologic deficit", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "neutral", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "no-generalized-rash", "region": "skin", "maneuver": "Inspect the trunk, back, and extremities", "finding": "No widespread vesicular or dermatomal rash", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "neutral", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "no-genital-lesions", "region": "genitourinary", "maneuver": "Ask about genital sores; examine only if symptoms are reported", "finding": "No genital vesicles or ulcers", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "neutral", "importance": "optional"}, {"id": "no-dehydration", "region": "general", "maneuver": "Assess mucous membranes, skin turgor, and ability to drink (especially in children)", "finding": "Moist mucous membranes, normal skin turgor, drinking well", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "neutral", "importance": "important"}]}, "vitals": {"temp": {"mild": {"min": 98.0, "max": 99.5}, "moderate": {"min": 99.0, "max": 100.8}, "severe": {"min": 100.0, "max": 102.5}, "critical": {"min": 101.0, "max": 104.0}}, "hr": {"moderate": {"min": 80, "max": 100}, "severe": {"min": 95, "max": 120}, "critical": {"min": 110, "max": 140}}, "rr": {"severe": {"min": 18, "max": 24}, "critical": {"min": 22, "max": 30}}, "sbp": {"critical": {"min": 80, "max": 100}}, "dbp": {"critical": {"min": 45, "max": 65}}}, "labs": [], "imaging": [], "workup": {"initial": [{"test": "Herpes simplex virus type-specific serology (HSV-1/HSV-2 IgG)", "why": "Confirms prior HSV infection when the diagnosis is uncertain; not needed for a classic recurrent lip sore", "only_if": "the diagnosis is unclear or the patient wants to know their HSV status"}, {"test": "Viral culture of the lesion", "why": "Detects HSV from an unroofed vesicle when the diagnosis is in doubt", "only_if": "the lesion is atypical, severe, or the patient is immunocompromised"}], "confirmatory": [{"test": "HSV polymerase chain reaction (PCR) of lesion swab", "why": "Most sensitive and specific test to confirm HSV when the clinical picture is atypical or the diagnosis matters", "only_if": "atypical, severe, or recurrent lesions where confirmation changes management"}], "supporting": [{"test": "cbc", "why": "Not routinely needed; may show a mild leukocytosis with a first episode or severe gingivostomatitis", "only_if": "severe first episode with fever or concern for a bacterial superinfection"}, {"test": "crp", "why": "Nonspecific; may be mildly elevated with a severe first episode", "only_if": "concern for bacterial superinfection or another inflammatory process"}], "not_indicated": [{"test": "cbc", "why": "A classic recurrent cold sore is diagnosed clinically; blood counts do not change management"}, {"test": "bmp", "why": "No role in an uncomplicated lip sore"}, {"test": "crp", "why": "Not needed for a typical cold sore"}, {"test": "esr", "why": "Not needed for a typical cold sore"}, {"test": "Herpes simplex virus type-specific serology (HSV-1/HSV-2 IgG)", "why": "Does not distinguish a current outbreak from past infection and is not needed for a classic presentation"}, {"test": "Viral culture of the lesion", "why": "Not routine for a classic recurrent cold sore; yield falls as the lesion crusts"}, {"test": "HSV polymerase chain reaction (PCR) of lesion swab", "why": "Not routine for a classic recurrent cold sore"}, {"test": "csf", "why": "Only if meningitis or encephalitis is suspected, which is not part of uncomplicated cold sores"}, {"test": "MRI brain without and with contrast", "why": "Only if herpes encephalitis is suspected"}], "decision_points": ["A classic recurrent vesicle at the lip margin is diagnosed clinically; testing is reserved for atypical, severe, or immunocompromised presentations", "Consider referral or urgent evaluation if there is eye involvement, widespread skin lesions, or neurologic symptoms", "In children, assess hydration and ability to drink; dehydration may require supportive care"], "criteria": [], "scores": []}, "differential": [{"name": "Herpetic whitlow", "slug": "herpetic-whitlow", "why_considered": "HSV infection of the finger can produce a painful vesicular lesion that may be mistaken for a cold sore when the patient reports a hand lesion or when the history is unclear.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Lesion on a finger or thumb, often after contact with a person who has a cold sore", "Occupational exposure such as dental or health care work", "No lip or perioral involvement"], "exam": ["Tense, painful vesicles on an erythematous base on the finger", "Local tenderness and swelling of the digit"], "labs": ["HSV PCR of the lesion can confirm if needed"], "imaging": []}}, {"name": "Herpes zoster ophthalmicus", "slug": "herpes-zoster", "why_considered": "A vesicular rash near the mouth or face could be confused with a cold sore, but zoster follows a dermatomal pattern and can involve the eye.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Vesicles in a dermatomal distribution, especially near the eye or forehead, or any eye redness or pain", "discriminators": {"history": ["Pain or tingling in a band-like distribution before the rash", "Rash on one side of the face, often forehead or cheek", "Eye redness, pain, or blurred vision if the ophthalmic branch is involved"], "exam": ["Vesicles grouped in a dermatomal pattern that does not cross the midline", "Conjunctival injection, corneal lesion, or decreased visual acuity if the eye is involved"], "labs": ["Varicella-zoster virus PCR of the lesion if the diagnosis is uncertain"], "imaging": []}}, {"name": "Aphthous ulcers (canker sores)", "slug": "aphthous-ulcers", "why_considered": "Painful mouth ulcers are a common complaint and can be mistaken for cold sores, especially when they occur inside the mouth.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Ulcers inside the mouth, often on the tongue, buccal mucosa, or soft palate", "No tingling or burning before the ulcer appears", "No vesicle stage; ulcers start as a round, shallow sore with a yellow-white center and red rim", "Not contagious; no known viral trigger"], "exam": ["Single or few round, shallow ulcers with a gray-white base and erythematous halo", "No lip or perioral vesicles"], "labs": [], "imaging": []}}, {"name": "Hand-foot-and-mouth disease", "slug": "hand-foot-and-mouth-disease", "why_considered": "Coxsackievirus infection can cause mouth sores and a rash, and in children it may present with fever and refusal to eat.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Fever, sore throat, and rash on hands and feet", "Recent contact with a child with similar symptoms", "Mouth sores are usually inside the mouth, not on the lip margin"], "exam": ["Vesicles or ulcers on the palms, soles, and sometimes buttocks", "Mouth ulcers on the tongue, gums, and buccal mucosa", "No lip margin vesicles"], "labs": ["Clinical diagnosis; viral PCR or serology rarely needed"], "imaging": []}}, {"name": "Herpangina", "slug": "herpangina", "why_considered": "Another enteroviral illness that causes fever and painful mouth ulcers, often in children.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Sudden fever, sore throat, and refusal to eat", "No lip tingling or cold sore history"], "exam": ["Small vesicles or ulcers on the soft palate, tonsils, and uvula", "No lip or perioral lesions"], "labs": [], "imaging": []}}, {"name": "Herpes simplex keratitis", "slug": "herpes-simplex-keratitis", "why_considered": "HSV can infect the eye and cause a painful red eye with a dendritic corneal lesion; this can occur with or without a cold sore.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Eye redness, pain, tearing, or blurred vision in a patient with a cold sore or recent HSV infection", "discriminators": {"history": ["Eye pain, redness, tearing, or photophobia", "Blurred vision or foreign body sensation", "May have a history of cold sores"], "exam": ["Conjunctival injection, corneal haze, or a dendritic ulcer on fluorescein staining", "Decreased visual acuity"], "labs": ["HSV PCR of corneal scraping if needed, but usually clinical"], "imaging": []}}, {"name": "Squamous cell carcinoma of the lip", "slug": "squamous-cell-carcinoma-lip", "why_considered": "A non-healing lip lesion can be mistaken for a cold sore, especially if it persists beyond the typical 2 weeks.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "A lip sore that does not heal within 3 weeks, is indurated, or bleeds easily", "discriminators": {"history": ["Lesion present for weeks to months and progressively enlarging", "No prodromal tingling or vesicle stage", "History of sun exposure, smoking, or alcohol use"], "exam": ["Indurated, ulcerated, or crusted lesion on the lip that may be painless", "May have palpable cervical lymph nodes"], "labs": [], "imaging": ["Biopsy is required for diagnosis; imaging of the neck may be considered if nodes are present"]}}], "course": {"severity": {"mild": "Prodromal tingling or a small cluster of vesicles at the lip margin; no fever, no systemic symptoms, able to eat and drink normally.", "moderate": "Painful vesicles or ulcers with local tenderness, mild fever, tender submandibular lymph nodes, and discomfort with eating; no dehydration.", "severe": "First episode with widespread oral ulcers (gingivostomatitis), fever, significant pain, difficulty eating or drinking, and possible mild dehydration; or recurrent outbreaks that are unusually large or frequent.", "critical": "Disseminated HSV infection with widespread skin lesions, hepatitis, pneumonitis, or central nervous system involvement (encephalitis or meningitis); this is rare and occurs mainly in immunocompromised patients or neonates."}, "red_flags": [{"finding": "Eye redness, pain, or blurred vision", "meaning": "Possible herpes simplex keratitis; urgent ophthalmology evaluation is needed to prevent corneal damage."}, {"finding": "Severe headache, confusion, neck stiffness, or seizures", "meaning": "Possible herpes encephalitis or meningitis; requires urgent evaluation and treatment."}, {"finding": "Widespread vesicles beyond the lip or mouth, especially in an immunocompromised patient", "meaning": "Possible disseminated HSV infection; needs urgent antiviral therapy."}, {"finding": "Inability to drink, drooling, or signs of dehydration in a child", "meaning": "Severe gingivostomatitis with dehydration; may require intravenous fluids and hospitalization."}, {"finding": "Lip sore that does not heal within 3 weeks or is indurated and bleeding", "meaning": "Possible squamous cell carcinoma; requires biopsy."}, {"finding": "Neonate with fever, poor feeding, lethargy, or vesicles", "meaning": "Possible neonatal HSV infection; a medical emergency requiring immediate evaluation and treatment."}], "complications": [{"name": "Herpes simplex keratitis", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Eye redness, pain, tearing, or blurred vision", "Dendritic corneal ulcer on fluorescein staining"]}, {"name": "Eczema herpeticum", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Widespread vesicular rash in a patient with atopic dermatitis", "Fever and malaise"]}, {"name": "Herpes encephalitis", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Fever, headache, confusion, seizures, or focal neurologic deficits", "Abnormal cerebrospinal fluid with lymphocytic pleocytosis"]}, {"name": "Dehydration from severe gingivostomatitis", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Inability to drink, drooling, decreased urine output", "Dry mucous membranes, tachycardia"]}, {"name": "Bacterial superinfection of the cold sore", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "minor", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Increasing redness, swelling, pain, or purulent drainage", "Fever"]}], "special_populations": [{"population": "children", "differences": "First episode often presents as gingivostomatitis with fever, irritability, swollen gums, multiple mouth ulcers, drooling, and refusal to eat. Assess hydration carefully; young children may need supportive care or hospitalization for fluids."}, {"population": "infants", "differences": "May present with fever, poor feeding, and irritability without a clear lip sore. Neonatal HSV infection can be severe and disseminated; any suspected neonatal infection requires urgent evaluation and treatment."}, {"population": "immunocompromised", "differences": "Sores may be larger, more numerous, slower to heal, and may spread to other skin areas or inside the mouth. Disseminated infection is more likely; antiviral therapy is often needed and may be given intravenously."}, {"population": "older_adults", "differences": "May have more frequent or slower-healing outbreaks. Other causes of mouth sores such as medication reactions, nutritional deficiencies, or malignancy are more likely and should be considered if the presentation is atypical."}, {"population": "pregnancy", "differences": "Primary HSV infection near delivery can pose a risk of neonatal transmission. Recurrent cold sores are usually not dangerous, but pregnant patients should inform their obstetrician if they have active lesions at the time of labor."}]}, "teaching": {"key_questions": [{"question": "What does the sore look like, and where is it?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "A cluster of small blisters or a crusted sore at the outer lip margin, corner of the mouth, or nostril"}, {"question": "Did you feel any tingling, itching, or burning before the sore appeared?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "A prodrome of tingling or burning at the lip hours to a day before the blister — supports HSV"}, {"question": "How long has it been there, and is it getting better or worse?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Blister then crust then healing over 7–14 days; a sore that persists beyond 3 weeks is a red flag"}, {"question": "Have you had sores like this before?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Recurrent episodes at the same lip location — strongly supports recurrent herpes labialis"}, {"question": "What seems to bring them on?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Sun or wind exposure, stress, fatigue, fever or another illness, menstruation, dental work"}, {"question": "Does it hurt, and does it hurt to eat or drink?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Tenderness and burning; pain with eating suggests a first episode with mouth ulcers"}, {"question": "Have you had a fever or felt generally unwell?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Fever and malaise with a first episode (gingivostomatitis); usually absent in recurrences"}, {"question": "Any swollen or tender glands in the neck or under the jaw?", "category": "associated", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Tender submandibular or cervical nodes, more common with a first episode"}, {"question": "Any redness, pain, tearing, or blurred vision in your eyes?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Eye involvement suggests herpes simplex keratitis and needs urgent ophthalmology evaluation"}, {"question": "Any severe headache, confusion, neck stiffness, or seizures?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Suggests herpes encephalitis or meningitis — a medical emergency"}, {"question": "Do you have any condition or take any medicine that weakens your immune system?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Immunosuppression raises the risk of larger, slower-healing, or disseminated lesions"}, {"question": "Do you take any medicines by mouth or on the skin, including steroids or other immunosuppressants?", "category": "medications", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Prednisone, tacrolimus, methotrexate, cyclosporine, azathioprine"}, {"question": "Do you have eczema or atopic dermatitis?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Atopic dermatitis raises the risk of eczema herpeticum if HSV spreads over the skin"}, {"question": "Has anyone close to you had a cold sore, and do you share drinks, utensils, or lip balm?", "category": "social", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Recent contact with an active cold sore; sharing items that touch the mouth"}, {"question": "Do you have any genital sores or blisters?", "category": "sexual", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Genital lesions suggest HSV-2 or genital herpes rather than a simple cold sore"}, {"question": "Any widespread rash, blisters elsewhere on the body, or skin peeling?", "category": "ros", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Widespread vesicles suggest disseminated HSV or another diagnosis such as eczema herpeticum"}, {"question": "Any unintentional weight loss, night sweats, or a sore that bleeds easily?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Constitutional symptoms or a non-healing, indurated, bleeding lip lesion raise concern for malignancy"}], "accepted_names": ["Cold sore", "Cold sores", "Herpes labialis", "Oral herpes", "Fever blister", "Fever blisters", "Recurrent herpes labialis", "HSV-1 infection", "Herpes simplex virus type 1 infection"], "reasoning_points": ["Recurrent episodes of a small cluster of vesicles at the outer lip margin or nostril", "A prodrome of tingling, itching, or burning at the lip before the blister appears", "The lesion progresses from vesicles to crusting and heals without a scar in about 1–2 weeks", "Triggers such as sun or wind exposure, stress, fever, or menstruation", "Typical lesions are diagnosed clinically; testing is reserved for atypical, severe, or immunocompromised presentations", "Red flags such as eye involvement, neurologic symptoms, widespread lesions, dehydration, or a sore that has not healed in 3 weeks are absent", "Look-alikes such as aphthous ulcers, hand-foot-and-mouth disease, herpes zoster, and squamous cell carcinoma of the lip are considered and excluded"], "difficulty": {"easy": ["Young adult with a recurrent tingling blister at the lip margin", "Clear prodrome followed by a cluster of vesicles that crusts and heals", "Known triggers such as sun exposure or stress", "No fever, no eye symptoms, no systemic illness"], "medium": ["First episode in a child with fever, swollen gums, multiple mouth ulcers, and refusal to eat", "Lesion at the corner of the mouth or nostril rather than the mid-lip", "Patient with atopic dermatitis and a few vesicles spreading onto the cheek", "Older adult with a slower-healing outbreak and other possible causes of mouth sores"], "hard": ["Immunocompromised patient with large, numerous, or slowly healing lesions", "Neonate or infant with fever, poor feeding, and irritability without a clear lip sore", "Lip lesion that has not healed in 3 weeks or is indurated and bleeding", "Patient with eye redness, pain, or blurred vision suggesting herpetic keratitis", "Severe gingivostomatitis with dehydration requiring supportive care"]}}}$rounds_kb$::jsonb)
