ng an oral glucose tolerance test, or a random plasma glucose of 200 mg/dL or higher in a patient with classic symptoms of hyperglycemia or a hyperglycemic crisis. In the absence of unequivocal symptoms, the same test should be repeated to confirm the result.", "items": ["A1C 6.5% or higher", "Fasting plasma glucose 126 mg/dL or higher", "2-hour plasma glucose 200 mg/dL or higher on oral glucose tolerance testing", "Random plasma glucose 200 mg/dL or higher with classic symptoms or hyperglycemic crisis"]}, {"name": "Prediabetes thresholds", "summary": "Prediabetes is present when glucose is above normal but below the diabetes cutoffs: A1C 5.7 to 6.4%, fasting plasma glucose 100 to 125 mg/dL, or a 2-hour plasma glucose of 140 to 199 mg/dL on oral glucose tolerance testing.", "items": ["A1C 5.7 to 6.4%", "Fasting plasma glucose 100 to 125 mg/dL", "2-hour plasma glucose 140 to 199 mg/dL"]}], "scores": ["framingham_risk", "ascvd_risk"]}, "differential": [{"name": "Type 1 diabetes", "slug": "type-1-diabetes", "why_considered": "Both cause hyperglycemia with thirst, urination and weight loss, and the distinction changes treatment completely.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Younger or lean patient, rapid symptom onset over weeks, or ketones and acidosis at presentation", "discriminators": {"history": ["Symptoms develop over weeks rather than months to years", "Weight loss is prominent despite normal or increased appetite", "Personal or family history of autoimmune disease such as thyroid disease or celiac disease"], "exam": ["Often lean or normal weight rather than overweight", "May appear ill or dehydrated if presenting in ketoacidosis"], "labs": ["Positive glutamic acid decarboxylase or islet antigen 2 antibodies", "Low or undetectable C-peptide", "Large urine ketones with a low bicarbonate and high anion gap"]}}, {"name": "Diabetic ketoacidosis", "slug": "diabetic-ketoacidosis", "why_considered": "Severe hyperglycemia with dehydration, vomiting and altered mental status can be the first presentation of type 2 diabetes, especially in Black and Hispanic adults.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Vomiting, deep rapid breathing, fruity breath, abdominal pain or drowsiness in a patient with very high glucose", "discriminators": {"history": ["Nausea and vomiting with abdominal pain", "Symptoms worsening over days rather than months", "Recent infection, missed medication or new steroid use"], "exam": ["Kussmaul respirations", "Dry mucous membranes and poor skin turgor", "Drowsiness or confusion"], "labs": ["Glucose usually above 250 mg/dL", "Bicarbonate below 18 mmol/L with an elevated anion gap", "Moderate to large urine or serum ketones", "pH below 7.30 on arterial or venous blood gas"]}}, {"name": "Hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state", "slug": "hyperosmolar-hyperglycemic-state", "why_considered": "Older patients with type 2 diabetes can present with extreme hyperglycemia, profound dehydration and altered mental status without significant ketosis.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Glucose often above 600 mg/dL with marked dehydration, confusion or focal neurologic deficits and minimal ketones", "discriminators": {"history": ["Days to weeks of worsening thirst and urination", "Often triggered by infection, myocardial infarction or stroke", "Lives in a nursing facility or has limited access to water"], "exam": ["Severe dehydration with dry mucous membranes and hypotension", "Altered mental status ranging from confusion to coma", "Focal neurologic deficits that resolve with fluid and insulin"], "labs": ["Serum osmolality above 320 mOsm/kg", "Glucose often above 600 mg/dL", "Bicarbonate usually above 18 mmol/L with minimal ketones", "Marked prerenal azotemia with elevated BUN and creatinine"]}}, {"name": "Medication-induced hyperglycemia", "slug": "medication-induced-hyperglycemia", "why_considered": "Glucocorticoids, atypical antipsychotics, thiazides and calcineurin inhibitors can raise glucose and mimic new type 2 diabetes.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Recent start or dose increase of prednisone, olanzapine, quetiapine, hydrochlorothiazide or tacrolimus", "Glucose rises after meals and in the afternoon, especially with steroids", "No classic osmotic symptoms before the medication was started"], "labs": ["Hyperglycemia that improves when the offending drug is reduced or stopped", "A1C may be normal if the exposure is recent"]}}, {"name": "Stress hyperglycemia", "slug": "stress-hyperglycemia", "why_considered": "Acute illness, surgery, trauma or infection can raise glucose in patients without underlying diabetes.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Glucose elevated during a hospital admission for another reason", "No prior history of diabetes or prediabetes", "No thirst, urination or weight loss before the illness"], "labs": ["A1C in the normal range", "Glucose normalizes as the acute illness resolves", "Catecholamine and cortisol surge pattern with elevated glucose only during stress"]}}, {"name": "Cushing syndrome", "slug": "cushing-syndrome", "why_considered": "Excess cortisol causes insulin resistance, central weight gain and hyperglycemia that can look like type 2 diabetes.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Progressive central weight gain with thinning arms and legs", "Easy bruising, purple abdominal striae and proximal muscle weakness", "Chronic glucocorticoid use is the most common cause"], "exam": ["Moon facies, buffalo hump and supraclavicular fat pads", "Proximal muscle weakness on rising from a chair"], "labs": ["Elevated late-night salivary cortisol or 24-hour urine free cortisol", "Failure to suppress cortisol on a low-dose dexamethasone suppression test"]}}, {"name": "Hypothyroidism", "slug": "hypothyroidism", "why_considered": "Fatigue, weight gain, dry skin and mild hyperglycemia overlap with type 2 diabetes and thyroid disease clusters with it.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Cold intolerance, constipation and heavy menstrual periods", "Weight gain rather than weight loss", "Slow speech and slowed thinking"], "exam": ["Dry coarse skin, bradycardia and delayed relaxation of deep tendon reflexes", "Diffuse goiter or thyroidectomy scar"], "labs": ["Elevated TSH with low free T4", "Positive thyroid peroxidase antibodies if autoimmune"]}}, {"name": "Pancreatic diabetes", "slug": "pancreatic-diabetes", "why_considered": "Chronic pancreatitis, pancreatic cancer or cystic fibrosis can destroy islet cells and cause hyperglycemia.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Chronic heavy alcohol use or recurrent pancreatitis", "Steatorrhea with greasy, floating stools", "Weight loss out of proportion to glucose elevation"], "exam": ["Epigastric tenderness or a palpable mass", "Signs of fat-soluble vitamin deficiency"], "labs": ["Low fecal elastase", "Elevated lipase or amylase during flares", "Low C-peptide with absent diabetes autoantibodies"], "imaging": ["Pancreatic calcifications on CT", "Pancreatic duct dilation on MRCP"]}}, {"name": "Monogenic diabetes", "slug": "monogenic-diabetes", "why_considered": "Maturity-onset diabetes of the young and neonatal diabetes can present with mild hyperglycemia in young, non-obese patients with a strong family history.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Diabetes in multiple generations with an autosomal dominant pattern", "Diagnosis before age 25 in a lean patient", "Mild fasting hyperglycemia that is stable for years"], "labs": ["Negative diabetes autoantibodies", "Preserved C-peptide", "Genetic panel identifies a specific mutation such as HNF1A or GCK"]}}, {"name": "Gestational diabetes", "slug": "gestational-diabetes", "why_considered": "Hyperglycemia first recognized during pregnancy is usually gestational diabetes and is often silent.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Diagnosis during the second or third trimester", "Prior baby weighing 9 pounds or more", "Prior gestational diabetes"], "labs": ["Abnormal 50 g glucose challenge followed by an abnormal 100 g oral glucose tolerance test", "A1C usually below 6.5%"]}}], "course": {"severity": {"mild": "A1C 6.5 to 7.0% on no medication or a single agent, no symptoms, no complications, normal kidney function and no albuminuria", "moderate": "A1C 7.0 to 8.5% despite lifestyle and one or two agents, early complications such as mild retinopathy, microalbuminuria or early neuropathy, and mild hypertension", "severe": "A1C 8.5 to 11.0% with osmotic symptoms, weight loss, established complications such as proliferative retinopathy, diabetic kidney disease or a non-healing foot ulcer, and often needs insulin", "critical": "A1C above 11.0% or a hyperglycemic emergency with dehydration, acidosis or altered mental status, or a life-threatening complication such as myocardial infarction, stroke or sepsis from an infected foot"}, "red_flags": [{"finding": "Vomiting, deep rapid breathing or fruity breath", "meaning": "Diabetic ketoacidosis; needs urgent fluids, insulin and electrolyte monitoring"}, {"finding": "Glucose above 600 mg/dL with confusion or focal neurologic deficits", "meaning": "Hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state; high mortality without prompt treatment"}, {"finding": "Chest pain, pressure or unexplained shortness of breath", "meaning": "Silent myocardial infarction is common in diabetes and may present without classic pain"}, {"finding": "Sudden one-sided weakness, facial droop or trouble speaking", "meaning": "Acute stroke; time-critical evaluation and treatment"}, {"finding": "A foot ulcer with spreading redness, foul drainage, crepitus or black tissue", "meaning": "Deep infection or necrotizing fasciitis; surgical emergency"}, {"finding": "Sudden painless vision loss or new floaters", "meaning": "Vitreous hemorrhage or retinal detachment from proliferative retinopathy"}, {"finding": "Hypotension, tachycardia or altered mental status", "meaning": "Sepsis, hypovolemia or adrenal insufficiency; needs immediate resuscitation"}], "complications": [{"name": "Diabetic ketoacidosis", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Vomiting and abdominal pain", "Kussmaul respirations", "Large urine ketones with low bicarbonate", "Often triggered by infection or missed insulin"]}, {"name": "Hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Glucose often above 600 mg/dL", "Serum osmolality above 320 mOsm/kg", "Profound dehydration and altered mental status", "Minimal ketones"]}, {"name": "Hypoglycemia", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Sweating, tremor, palpitations and hunger", "Confusion or seizure with severe lows", "Often from insulin or sulfonylureas, missed meals or exercise"]}, {"name": "Diabetic retinopathy", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Microaneurysms and dot-blot hemorrhages on dilated exam", "Blurred vision or floaters", "Long duration of diabetes and poor control"]}, {"name": "Diabetic kidney disease", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Persistent albuminuria on urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio", "Rising creatinine and falling eGFR", "Coexisting hypertension and retinopathy"]}, {"name": "Peripheral neuropathy", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Symmetric stocking-distribution numbness, tingling or burning", "Loss of vibration sense and ankle reflexes", "Loss of protective sensation on monofilament testing"]}, {"name": "Diabetic foot ulcer and infection", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Non-healing ulcer at a pressure point", "Callus, deformity and dry fissured skin", "Diminished pedal pulses or loss of sensation"]}, {"name": "Peripheral arterial disease", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Claudication or rest pain", "Absent pedal pulses", "Reduced ankle-brachial index"]}, {"name": "Coronary artery disease and myocardial infarction", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["May be silent or present with atypical symptoms", "ST depression or T-wave changes on ECG", "Coexisting hypertension and hyperlipidemia"]}, {"name": "Stroke", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Sudden focal neurologic deficit", "Carotid bruit", "Prior transient ischemic attack"]}, {"name": "Autonomic neuropathy", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Orthostatic hypotension", "Gastroparesis with early satiety and vomiting", "Erectile dysfunction and bladder dysfunction"]}, {"name": "Nonalcoholic fatty liver disease", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "minor", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Mildly elevated ALT and AST", "Increased hepatic echogenicity on ultrasound", "Central obesity and metabolic syndrome"]}, {"name": "Infections", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Recurrent urinary tract infections", "Candidal infections of skin and genitals", "Poor wound healing"]}, {"name": "Cataracts and glaucoma", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "minor", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Progressive blurry vision", "Lens opacity on exam", "Elevated intraocular pressure"]}], "special_populations": [{"population": "children", "differences": "Type 2 diabetes in children and teens usually comes with obesity and a strong family history; they may have no symptoms and be found on screening, or present with acanthosis nigricans and high blood pressure. Metformin and lifestyle change are first-line, and the disease progresses faster than in adults."}, {"population": "older_adults", "differences": "Symptoms are often blamed on aging; thirst may be blunted, and they may present with confusion, falls, poor wound healing or a hyperglycemic emergency. A1C targets are often relaxed to avoid hypoglycemia, and sulfonylureas and insulin are used cautiously."}, {"population": "pregnancy", "differences": "High blood sugar first found in pregnancy is usually gestational diabetes; it is often silent and picked up on routine screening, and it raises the risk of later type 2 diabetes. Tight control reduces macrosomia and shoulder dystocia, and insulin is preferred when medication is needed."}, {"population": "obesity", "differences": "Patients with obesity may have insulin resistance and acanthosis nigricans for years before glucose rises; symptoms stay mild and the diagnosis is often made on screening labs. Weight loss of 5 to 10% can improve glucose substantially, and GLP-1 receptor agonists and bariatric surgery are options."}, {"population": "renal_impairment", "differences": "Metformin is avoided when eGFR is below 30 mL/min/1.73 m2 and dose-reduced at 30 to 45; many other agents need dose adjustment, and insulin requirements fall as kidney function declines."}, {"population": "immunocompromised", "differences": "Glucocorticoids and calcineurin inhibitors used for immunosuppression raise glucose; infections are more severe and atypical, and glucose control is harder during treatment."}]}, "teaching": {"key_questions": [{"question": "Have you been more thirsty than usual?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Constant, hard-to-satisfy thirst, often worse at night"}, {"question": "How often are you urinating, including at night?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Frequent urination, especially waking at night to urinate"}, {"question": "How have your energy levels been?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Persistent tiredness or low energy, often attributed to aging"}, {"question": "Have you lost weight without trying?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Unintentional weight loss over months"}, {"question": "How long have these symptoms been going on, and did they come on slowly or suddenly?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Gradual onset over months to years; sudden onset over days suggests type 1 diabetes or ketoacidosis"}, {"question": "Has your vision been blurry or changing?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Blurred vision that fluctuates with glucose levels"}, {"question": "Any numbness, tingling or burning in your feet or hands?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Symmetric stocking-distribution sensory symptoms suggesting neuropathy"}, {"question": "Have you had any sores or cuts that are slow to heal?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Poor wound healing, especially on the feet"}, {"question": "Have you had frequent yeast infections or urinary tract infections?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Recurrent candidal or urinary infections"}, {"question": "Any vomiting, deep rapid breathing, fruity-smelling breath or severe abdominal pain?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Features of diabetic ketoacidosis or a hyperglycemic emergency"}, {"question": "Any chest pain, pressure or unexplained shortness of breath?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Silent myocardial infarction, which is common in diabetes"}, {"question": "Any sudden weakness on one side, facial droop or trouble speaking?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Acute stroke"}, {"question": "Do you have high blood pressure, high cholesterol, heart disease or a prior stroke?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Cardiovascular risk factors and metabolic syndrome"}, {"question": "Have you ever been told your blood sugar was high, or that you had prediabetes or gestational diabetes?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Prior prediabetes, gestational diabetes or a baby weighing 9 pounds or more"}, {"question": "Do you take any steroids, antipsychotics, thiazide diuretics or transplant medicines?", "category": "medications", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Medications that can raise glucose such as prednisone, olanzapine, quetiapine, hydrochlorothiazide or tacrolimus"}, {"question": "Does anyone in your family have diabetes?", "category": "family", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Type 2 diabetes in a parent or sibling"}, {"question": "How much do you exercise, and what does a typical day of eating look like?", "category": "social", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Sedentary lifestyle, high intake of refined carbohydrates and sugar-sweetened drinks"}, {"question": "Have you had any trouble getting or keeping an erection?", "category": "sexual", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Erectile dysfunction as a sign of vascular or neuropathic complications"}], "accepted_names": ["Type 2 diabetes", "Type 2 diabetes mellitus", "T2DM", "Diabetes mellitus type 2", "Adult-onset diabetes", "Non-insulin-dependent diabetes mellitus", "NIDDM"], "reasoning_points": ["Gradual onset over months to years of thirst, frequent urination and fatigue in an adult with risk factors", "Overweight or obesity with central adiposity, and sometimes acanthosis nigricans", "A1C of 6.5% or higher, or fasting plasma glucose of 126 mg/dL or higher, or a random glucose of 200 mg/dL or higher with classic symptoms", "An abnormal screening result is confirmed with a repeat test unless the patient has unequivocal symptoms with a glucose of 200 mg/dL or higher", "Type 1 diabetes is considered and excluded when the patient is young, lean, or has ketones and acidosis", "Complications are assessed at diagnosis, including retinopathy, kidney disease, neuropathy and cardiovascular risk"], "difficulty": {"easy": ["Middle-aged adult with obesity and a family history of diabetes", "Classic thirst, frequent urination and fatigue over months", "A1C well above 6.5% with a fasting glucose above 126 mg/dL", "Acanthosis nigricans on exam"], "medium": ["No symptoms, diagnosis made on routine screening labs", "Older adult whose thirst and fatigue are blamed on aging", "Recurrent yeast infections or a non-healing foot sore as the presenting complaint", "Mild hyperglycemia with a normal A1C due to anemia or recent transfusion"], "hard": ["Young, lean patient who could have type 1 diabetes or monogenic diabetes", "Glucose above 600 mg/dL with confusion and profound dehydration suggesting hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state", "Vomiting and deep rapid breathing suggesting ketoacidosis as the first presentation", "Hyperglycemia that appears only after starting prednisone or an atypical antipsychotic", "Pregnant patient with silent hyperglycemia found on routine screening"]}}}$rounds_kb$::jsonb)
