ce of embolus or structural disease", "only_if": "syncope, murmur, or suspected cardioembolic source"}], "not_indicated": [{"test": "CT head without IV contrast", "why": "Not needed for typical positional vertigo with a normal neurologic exam; CT is often normal in posterior circulation stroke"}, {"test": "MRI brain with and without IV contrast", "why": "Not routine for classic BPPV that resolves with a repositioning maneuver"}, {"test": "Carotid duplex ultrasound", "why": "No role in isolated vertigo without focal neurologic symptoms"}, {"test": "Transthoracic echocardiogram", "why": "Not routine unless a cardiac source is suspected"}, {"test": "Cervical spine x-ray", "why": "Rarely changes management of dizziness"}, {"test": "d_dimer", "why": "Not useful in dizziness without features of pulmonary embolism or dissection"}, {"test": "lactate", "why": "Not helpful unless sepsis or ischemia is suspected"}, {"test": "csf_opening", "why": "Reserved for suspected meningitis or subarachnoid hemorrhage, not routine dizziness"}], "decision_points": ["Use the HINTS exam (head impulse, nystagmus type, test of skew) in patients with acute continuous vertigo and vascular risk factors to separate peripheral from central causes", "Perform the Dix-Hallpike maneuver before imaging in patients with brief positional vertigo; a positive test with typical nystagmus confirms BPPV and needs no imaging", "Check orthostatic vital signs in every patient before attributing dizziness to the inner ear", "Pregnancy test before CT or medication changes in anyone who could be pregnant", "Sudden severe dizziness with focal deficits, new severe headache, or inability to walk needs urgent imaging rather than a vestibular suppressant"], "criteria": [], "scores": ["hints"]}, "differential": [{"name": "Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo", "slug": "benign-paroxysmal-positional-vertigo", "why_considered": "Brief spinning spells triggered by rolling over in bed or looking up, the most common cause of true vertigo", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Episodes last seconds to under a minute", "Triggered by specific head positions such as rolling over or bending forward", "No hearing loss, tinnitus, or ear fullness", "No focal neurologic symptoms"], "exam": ["Dix-Hallpike maneuver reproduces vertigo with a brief latency and upbeat-torsional nystagmus that fatigues on repeat testing", "Normal cranial nerves, strength, and gait between spells"], "labs": ["Not needed; diagnosis is clinical"], "imaging": ["Not indicated when the Dix-Hallpike maneuver is classic and the neurologic exam is normal"]}}, {"name": "Vestibular neuritis", "slug": "vestibular-neuritis", "why_considered": "Sudden, continuous vertigo with nausea and unsteady gait, often after a viral illness", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Abrupt onset of continuous vertigo lasting days", "Nausea and vomiting prominent", "No hearing loss", "Recent upper respiratory infection"], "exam": ["Unidirectional horizontal nystagmus suppressed by visual fixation", "Abnormal head impulse test with a corrective saccade on the affected side", "Normal test of skew and no focal neurologic deficits"], "labs": ["Not routinely needed; glucose and basic labs to exclude mimics"], "imaging": ["MRI brain if a central cause cannot be excluded by the HINTS exam"]}}, {"name": "Posterior circulation stroke", "slug": "posterior-circulation-stroke", "why_considered": "Acute vertigo can be the presenting symptom of cerebellar or brainstem infarction", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Acute continuous vertigo with vascular risk factors, new severe headache, or any focal neurologic finding", "discriminators": {"history": ["Sudden onset of continuous vertigo", "New severe headache or neck pain", "Diplopia, dysarthria, dysphagia, or numbness", "Vascular risk factors such as hypertension, diabetes, or atrial fibrillation"], "exam": ["Direction-changing or vertical nystagmus not suppressed by fixation", "Normal head impulse test with an abnormal test of skew", "Truncal ataxia or inability to sit or walk unsupported", "Focal cranial nerve, motor, or sensory deficits"], "labs": ["Glucose and basic metabolic panel to exclude hypoglycemia and electrolyte causes"], "imaging": ["MRI brain with diffusion-weighted imaging is the preferred test and may be needed more than once if initially negative", "CT head is often normal in acute posterior circulation stroke", "CT angiography of the head and neck if dissection or large vessel occlusion is suspected"], "ecg": ["Atrial fibrillation or other arrhythmia suggesting a cardioembolic source"]}}, {"name": "Meniere disease", "slug": "menieres-disease", "why_considered": "Recurrent spontaneous vertigo attacks with ear symptoms in a middle-aged adult", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Episodes lasting 20 minutes to several hours", "Unilateral fluctuating hearing loss", "Tinnitus and a sense of ear fullness on the affected side", "Attacks are spontaneous rather than strictly positional"], "exam": ["Asymmetric hearing on bedside screen or audiometry", "Nystagmus during attacks", "Normal neurologic exam between attacks"], "labs": ["Audiometry shows low-frequency sensorineural hearing loss"], "imaging": ["MRI of the internal auditory canals to exclude retrocochlear pathology"]}}, {"name": "Vestibular migraine", "slug": "vestibular-migraine", "why_considered": "Recurrent vertigo in a patient with a migraine history, especially a younger woman", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Personal or family history of migraine", "Episodes lasting minutes to days", "Headache, photophobia, or phonophobia during or around episodes", "No hearing loss"], "exam": ["Normal neurologic and vestibular examination between episodes", "No positional nystagmus on Dix-Hallpike"], "labs": ["Not diagnostic; used to exclude other causes"], "imaging": ["MRI brain if the presentation is atypical or red flags are present"]}}, {"name": "Orthostatic hypotension", "slug": "orthostatic-hypotension", "why_considered": "Lightheadedness on standing is a very common cause of dizziness, especially in older adults on multiple medications", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Symptoms occur on standing up, not with head position changes", "Medications such as diuretics, alpha-blockers, or antidepressants", "Diabetes with autonomic neuropathy", "No true spinning sensation"], "exam": ["Drop in systolic blood pressure of at least 20 mm Hg or diastolic of at least 10 mm Hg within 3 minutes of standing, with symptoms", "Heart rate rise over 30 bpm suggests volume depletion"], "labs": ["BUN-to-creatinine ratio above 20 supports dehydration", "Low hemoglobin suggests anemia"], "ecg": ["Sinus tachycardia or bradycardia; arrhythmia may contribute"]}}, {"name": "Cardiac arrhythmia", "slug": "cardiac-arrhythmia", "why_considered": "Palpitations with lightheadedness or near-fainting suggests a cardiac cause of cerebral hypoperfusion", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Palpitations, syncope or near-syncope, or an irregular pulse", "discriminators": {"history": ["Palpitations preceding the dizziness", "Near-fainting or fainting", "Known heart disease or heart failure", "No positional trigger"], "exam": ["Irregularly irregular pulse or bradycardia", "Murmur suggesting valvular disease", "Signs of heart failure"], "labs": ["Basic metabolic panel for electrolyte abnormalities", "TSH for thyroid disease"], "ecg": ["Atrial fibrillation with irregularly irregular response", "Sinus bradycardia or high-grade atrioventricular block", "Ischemic ST changes"], "imaging": ["Transthoracic echocardiogram if structural disease or a cardioembolic source is suspected"]}}, {"name": "Hypoglycemia", "slug": "hypoglycemia", "why_considered": "Lightheadedness, sweating, and confusion in a patient on insulin or a sulfonylurea", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Diabetes on insulin or a sulfonylurea with sweating and confusion", "discriminators": {"history": ["Missed meal, increased activity, or excess insulin dose", "Symptoms improve with eating", "No true spinning vertigo"], "exam": ["Diaphoresis, tremor, confusion, or altered mental status", "Symptoms resolve after glucose correction"], "labs": ["Bedside glucose below 70 mg/dL, often below 54 mg/dL with neuroglycopenic symptoms"]}}, {"name": "Anemia", "slug": "anemia", "why_considered": "Chronic lightheadedness and unsteadiness with fatigue, common in older adults", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Gradual onset of fatigue and exertional lightheadedness", "Heavy menstrual bleeding, gastrointestinal blood loss, or chronic kidney disease", "No positional or spinning component"], "exam": ["Pallor, tachycardia, flow murmur"], "labs": ["Low hemoglobin; iron studies and stool testing to find the source"]}}, {"name": "Giant cell arteritis", "slug": "giant-cell-arteritis", "why_considered": "Dizziness with headache in an older adult can signal cranial arteritis with risk of vision loss", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Age over 50 with new headache, jaw claudication, or visual symptoms", "discriminators": {"history": ["New headache, scalp tenderness, jaw claudication", "Visual blurring or transient vision loss", "Polymyalgia symptoms with shoulder and hip girdle pain"], "exam": ["Tender or nodular temporal artery with reduced pulsation", "Visual field defect"], "labs": ["Markedly elevated ESR and CRP", "Normocytic anemia and thrombocytosis"], "imaging": ["Temporal artery biopsy is the confirmatory test; vascular ultrasound of the temporal arteries may support the diagnosis"]}}, {"name": "Medication adverse effect", "slug": "medication-adverse-effect", "why_considered": "Many common medications cause dizziness, especially with polypharmacy in older adults", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Temporal link between a new or increased medication and symptoms", "Antihypertensives, sedative-hypnotics, anticholinergics, or opioids", "Symptoms worse after doses or on standing"], "exam": ["Orthostatic changes, sedation, or gait unsteadiness", "Normal vestibular and neurologic examination"], "labs": ["Electrolyte abnormalities from diuretics", "Renal function to guide dose adjustment"]}}], "course": {"severity": {"mild": "Intermittent, brief episodes with no interference in daily activities; normal gait and vital signs; patient is comfortable", "moderate": "Frequent or persistent symptoms with nausea and unsteady gait; patient avoids driving or needs to sit down; orthostatic changes may be present", "severe": "Disabling continuous vertigo with vomiting, inability to walk without support, and dehydration; focal findings may be absent but the patient cannot function", "critical": "Vertigo with focal neurologic deficits, inability to stand or protect the airway, or hemodynamic instability suggesting stroke, arrhythmia, or central nervous system infection"}, "red_flags": [{"finding": "Sudden severe dizziness with weakness, numbness, slurred speech, or double vision", "meaning": "Possible posterior circulation stroke; needs urgent imaging"}, {"finding": "New severe headache unlike any before, or neck pain", "meaning": "Hemorrhage, dissection, or central nervous system infection"}, {"finding": "Inability to walk or stand unsupported", "meaning": "Truncal ataxia suggests a central cause"}, {"finding": "Vertical or direction-changing nystagmus, or nystagmus that does not suppress with fixation", "meaning": "Central rather than peripheral vestibular lesion"}, {"finding": "Fever with neck stiffness or confusion", "meaning": "Meningitis or encephalitis"}, {"finding": "Palpitations with syncope or near-syncope", "meaning": "Arrhythmia causing cerebral hypoperfusion"}, {"finding": "Age over 50 with new headache, jaw claudication, or visual symptoms", "meaning": "Giant cell arteritis with risk of permanent vision loss"}, {"finding": "Recent head trauma or anticoagulant use", "meaning": "Intracranial hemorrhage"}], "complications": [{"name": "Falls and fractures", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Older adult with unsteady gait", "Osteoporosis", "Prior falls"]}, {"name": "Dehydration and acute kidney injury", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Persistent vomiting", "Poor oral intake", "Rising creatinine"]}, {"name": "Anxiety and activity avoidance", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "minor", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Fear of leaving home", "Avoidance of driving or crowded places", "Persistent symptoms after the original cause resolves"]}, {"name": "Permanent hearing loss", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Recurrent attacks with fluctuating hearing loss", "Progressive unilateral hearing decline"]}, {"name": "Stroke from missed posterior circulation ischemia", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Focal deficits", "Vascular risk factors", "Persistent vertigo with central nystagmus"]}], "special_populations": [{"population": "older_adults", "differences": "Multiple causes often coexist, including medications, orthostatic hypotension, vestibular loss, and neuropathy; falls and confusion may be the presenting complaint rather than vertigo."}, {"population": "children", "differences": "Young children cannot describe spinning and present with irritability, unsteady gait, or vomiting; migraine equivalents and inner ear infections are more prominent in the differential."}, {"population": "pregnancy", "differences": "Dizziness is common from physiologic changes; orthostatic hypotension, anemia, and hyperemesis must be considered, and medication choices are limited."}, {"population": "diabetes", "differences": "Autonomic neuropathy causes orthostatic dizziness, and hypoglycemia from insulin or sulfonylureas can present with lightheadedness and sweating."}, {"population": "renal_impairment", "differences": "Electrolyte abnormalities and medication accumulation increase dizziness; adjust doses of vestibular suppressants and avoid nephrotoxic agents."}, {"population": "immunocompromised", "differences": "Higher risk of central nervous system infection or malignancy presenting with dizziness, and symptoms may be blunted or atypical."}]}, "teaching": {"key_questions": [{"question": "When you say dizzy, do you mean the room is spinning, or that you feel lightheaded or unsteady?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "True vertigo (spinning) points to a vestibular cause; lightheadedness or unsteadiness points to orthostasis, cardiac, metabolic, or gait causes"}, {"question": "How long does each episode last?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Seconds to under a minute suggests BPPV; minutes to hours suggests Meniere disease or vestibular migraine; days of continuous symptoms suggests vestibular neuritis or stroke"}, {"question": "What brings it on?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Rolling over in bed or looking up suggests BPPV; standing up suggests orthostatic hypotension; spontaneous onset suggests neuritis, Meniere, or migraine"}, {"question": "Did it start suddenly, and is it constant or does it come and go?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Abrupt continuous vertigo lasting days suggests vestibular neuritis or posterior circulation stroke; recurrent discrete spells suggest BPPV, Meniere, or migraine"}, {"question": "Do you have any hearing loss, ringing, or fullness in one ear?", "category": "associated", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Unilateral fluctuating hearing loss with tinnitus and fullness suggests Meniere disease; isolated hearing loss raises concern for a retrocochlear lesion"}, {"question": "Do you have a headache, and do you have a history of migraines?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Migraine history with recurrent vertigo episodes suggests vestibular migraine"}, {"question": "Any weakness, numbness, trouble speaking, double vision, or trouble swallowing?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Focal neurologic symptoms suggest a central cause such as posterior circulation stroke"}, {"question": "Did you have a new, severe headache or neck pain with this?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Suggests hemorrhage, vertebral artery dissection, or central nervous system infection"}, {"question": "Can you walk on your own, or do you need help?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Inability to stand or walk unsupported suggests truncal ataxia from a central cause"}, {"question": "Any palpitations, chest pain, or fainting?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Suggests a cardiac arrhythmia or structural heart disease causing cerebral hypoperfusion"}, {"question": "Have you had a recent cold, flu, or ear infection?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "A recent upper respiratory infection supports vestibular neuritis"}, {"question": "Have you had any head injury or ear surgery?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Prior trauma or surgery predisposes to BPPV and other vestibular injury"}, {"question": "What medications do you take, and have any changed recently?", "category": "medications", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Diuretics, antihypertensives, alpha-blockers, antidepressants, sedative-hypnotics, opioids, and anticholinergics can all cause dizziness"}, {"question": "Do you have diabetes, and are you on insulin or a sulfonylurea?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Hypoglycemia is a reversible cause of lightheadedness, sweating, and confusion"}, {"question": "How much are you drinking, and have you been vomiting?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Poor intake or vomiting suggests dehydration and orthostatic dizziness"}, {"question": "Could you be pregnant? When was your last period?", "category": "obstetric", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Pregnancy changes the differential and the imaging and medication plan"}, {"question": "Do you drink alcohol, and how much?", "category": "social", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Heavy use contributes to dehydration, neuropathy, and unsteadiness"}, {"question": "Any fever, neck stiffness, or confusion?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Suggests meningitis or encephalitis"}], "accepted_names": ["Dizziness and vertigo", "Dizziness", "Vertigo", "Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo", "BPPV", "Vestibular neuritis", "Meniere disease", "Vestibular migraine", "Orthostatic hypotension", "Posterior circulation stroke"], "reasoning_points": ["Characterize the symptom as true spinning vertigo versus lightheadedness or unsteadiness, since this drives the differential", "Use episode duration and triggers to narrow the cause: seconds with position change for BPPV, minutes to hours for Meniere or migraine, days of continuous symptoms for neuritis or stroke", "Ask about ear symptoms because unilateral fluctuating hearing loss, tinnitus, and fullness point to Meniere disease", "Screen for focal neurologic symptoms, new severe headache, and inability to walk, which suggest a central cause", "Review medications and check orthostatic vital signs before attributing symptoms to the inner ear", "Check a bedside glucose in any patient on insulin or a sulfonylurea", "Use the HINTS exam in acute continuous vertigo to separate peripheral from central causes", "Perform the Dix-Hallpike maneuver before imaging when the history suggests brief positional vertigo", "Consider cardiac causes when palpitations, syncope, or an irregular pulse are present", "Consider giant cell arteritis in an older adult with new headache, jaw claudication, or visual symptoms"], "difficulty": {"easy": ["Classic BPPV: brief spinning spells triggered by rolling over in bed, positive Dix-Hallpike with fatigable upbeat-torsional nystagmus, normal neurologic exam", "Clear orthostatic dizziness on standing in a patient on a diuretic, with a documented blood pressure drop", "Younger patient with recurrent vertigo and a known migraine history"], "medium": ["Vestibular neuritis with days of continuous vertigo, unidirectional nystagmus suppressed by fixation, and an abnormal head impulse test", "Meniere disease with fluctuating unilateral hearing loss, tinnitus, and ear fullness between attacks", "Older adult on multiple medications with overlapping orthostatic and vestibular contributions", "Patient with anemia or hypothyroidism presenting with gradual unsteadiness rather than spinning"], "hard": ["Posterior circulation stroke presenting as isolated vertigo with a normal head impulse test and abnormal skew deviation", "Older adult with new headache, jaw claudication, and dizziness from giant cell arteritis", "Patient with hypoglycemia on insulin presenting with lightheadedness and confusion", "Child who cannot describe vertigo and presents with irritability, vomiting, and unsteady gait", "Persistent symptoms after the original cause resolves, with anxiety and activity avoidance"]}}}$rounds_kb$::jsonb)
