 of hemolytic uremic syndrome after Shiga toxin-producing E. coli; may present with irritability, poor feeding, and bloody diarrhea."}, {"population": "older_adults", "differences": "Higher risk of severe dehydration, bloodstream infection, and death; may have milder symptoms but more rapid progression."}, {"population": "pregnancy", "differences": "Urinary tract infection can ascend to pyelonephritis and trigger preterm labor; antibiotics must be chosen carefully."}, {"population": "immunocompromised", "differences": "Higher risk of invasive disease and sepsis; may have less prominent gastrointestinal symptoms."}]}, "teaching": {"key_questions": [{"question": "When did the diarrhea start, and how many times a day are you going?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Abrupt onset 1-3 days after exposure; frequent stools; duration under 10 days"}, {"question": "Is there blood or mucus in the stool?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Bloody diarrhea suggests Shiga toxin-producing E. coli and risk of hemolytic uremic syndrome"}, {"question": "Did the vomiting start before or after the diarrhea?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Vomiting after diarrhea; vomiting first suggests viral gastroenteritis"}, {"question": "What have you eaten in the last few days, especially ground beef, unpasteurized milk or juice, or raw produce?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Undercooked ground beef, unpasteurized dairy or juice, unwashed vegetables or sprouts"}, {"question": "Have you had contact with farm animals, petting zoos, or someone with diarrhea?", "category": "social", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Animal contact or a sick contact with similar illness"}, {"question": "Have you traveled recently, especially to an area with poor sanitation?", "category": "social", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Recent travel consistent with travelers' diarrhea"}, {"question": "Do you have burning with urination, going more often, or urgency?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Dysuria, frequency, urgency suggesting urinary tract infection"}, {"question": "Do you have pain in your back or side, fever, or chills?", "category": "associated", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Flank pain with fever and chills suggests pyelonephritis"}, {"question": "How much are you drinking, and how much urine are you making?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Poor intake or decreased urine output suggests dehydration or kidney injury"}, {"question": "Have you taken antibiotics recently, or been in the hospital?", "category": "medications", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Recent antibiotics or hospitalization for resistant or healthcare-associated strains"}, {"question": "Do you take immunosuppressants, steroids, or proton pump inhibitors?", "category": "medications", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Immunosuppression or acid suppression raising risk of invasive disease"}, {"question": "Do you have diabetes, kidney disease, or a weakened immune system?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Comorbidities that raise the risk of severe or invasive infection"}, {"question": "Do you use a urinary catheter or have you had urinary procedures?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Catheter or instrumentation as a source of urinary infection"}, {"question": "Could you be pregnant? When was your last period?", "category": "obstetric", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Pregnancy changes antibiotic choice and raises risk of ascending infection"}, {"question": "Any new sexual partner or recent sexual activity?", "category": "sexual", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Sexual activity as a risk factor for urinary tract infection"}, {"question": "Any rash, bruising, or unusual bleeding?", "category": "ros", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Petechiae or bruising suggesting thrombocytopenia from hemolytic uremic syndrome"}, {"question": "Any confusion, lightheadedness, or fainting?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Altered mental status or hypotension suggesting sepsis or severe dehydration"}], "accepted_names": ["E. coli infection", "Escherichia coli infection", "E. coli", "Escherichia coli", "E. coli gastroenteritis", "E. coli urinary tract infection", "E. coli UTI", "Shiga toxin-producing E. coli infection", "STEC infection", "E. coli O157:H7 infection", "E. coli sepsis", "E. coli bacteremia"], "reasoning_points": ["Acute diarrheal illness or urinary symptoms with a compatible exposure history", "Watery diarrhea that may become bloody, with crampy abdominal pain and low-grade fever", "Dysuria, frequency, urgency, or flank pain suggesting urinary tract infection", "Risk factors such as undercooked ground beef, unpasteurized products, animal contact, female anatomy, or catheter use", "Leukocytosis with neutrophil predominance and possible dehydration on labs", "Urinalysis with leukocyte esterase, nitrites, and white blood cells in urinary infection", "Stool culture or Shiga toxin testing identifies the organism in diarrheal illness", "Urine culture confirms E. coli urinary tract infection", "Blood cultures positive in invasive or bloodstream infection", "Complications such as hemolytic uremic syndrome, dehydration, pyelonephritis, or sepsis considered"], "difficulty": {"easy": ["Young child with bloody diarrhea after eating undercooked ground beef", "Classic watery then bloody diarrhea with severe cramps", "Urinalysis with positive nitrites and leukocyte esterase in a woman with dysuria", "Leukocytosis with left shift"], "medium": ["Older adult with mild diarrhea and dehydration but no fever", "Pregnant woman with urinary symptoms and risk of ascending infection", "Patient with recent antibiotic use and resistant organism", "Diarrhea with no clear food exposure"], "hard": ["Infant with irritability, poor feeding, and bloody diarrhea progressing to hemolytic uremic syndrome", "Immunocompromised patient with sepsis and minimal gastrointestinal symptoms", "Older adult with confusion and hypotension from E. coli bloodstream infection", "Bloody diarrhea followed by pallor, decreased urine output, and thrombocytopenia"]}}}$rounds_kb$::jsonb)
